COURSE_DATA["sen419"]={topics:[
{id:'t1',title:'UI/UX and front-end development',why:'UI vs UX, design principles and the front-end stack.',
cards:[
{h:'UI vs UX',b:`<table><tr><th>UI</th><th>UX</th></tr><tr><td>The look: layout, colour, typography, buttons</td><td>The whole experience: ease, efficiency, satisfaction</td></tr></table><p>UX process: research → personas → user flows → wireframes → prototypes → usability testing → iterate.</p><p>Principles: consistency, visual hierarchy, feedback, affordance, accessibility (WCAG: contrast, alt text, keyboard use), responsive design.</p>`},
{h:'Front-end stack',b:`<p><b>HTML</b> = structure · <b>CSS</b> = presentation (Flexbox, Grid, media queries) · <b>JavaScript</b> = behaviour (DOM, events, fetch). Frameworks: React (components, props, state, virtual DOM), Vue, Angular. Tooling: npm, Vite.</p>`},
{h:'UI vs UX on the slides',b:`<table><tr><th>User Experience (UX)</th><th>User Interface (UI)</th></tr><tr><td>How a product <b>feels</b> to use</td><td>How a product <b>looks</b> visually</td></tr><tr><td>Flow, logic and task completion</td><td>Colors, typography and spacing</td></tr><tr><td>Research, personas, journeys</td><td>Buttons, cards, icons and layout</td></tr><tr><td>Wireframing and prototyping</td><td>Animations and micro-interactions</td></tr><tr><td>Usability testing and iteration</td><td>Design systems and style guides</td></tr></table>`},
{h:'Visibility vs feedback',b:`<div class="def"><b>Visibility (passive)</b>The system's current state is readable at any moment without the user doing anything (for example, a Jumia cart badge showing "3 items"). Status, feedback and affordances are visible at a glance.</div><div class="def"><b>Feedback (reactive)</b>A direct system response to a user action: hover states, press animations, loading spinners, success or error messages. Silence is confusing.</div><p>Slide login-form example: add a <b>View password</b> toggle so users get immediate visual confirmation of what they typed.</p>`},
{h:'Consistency and hierarchy',b:`<p><b>Consistency:</b> same actions produce the same outcomes. Inconsistency is a bug: it breaks trust and creates cognitive load (solved by design systems).</p><p><b>Hierarchy:</b> not everything is equally important; visual weight guides the eye from <b>primary → secondary → tertiary</b> (anti-example: userinyerface.com).</p><p><b>Three visual cues for hierarchy:</b></p><ul><li><b>Font size and weight:</b> large, bold text for headings and calls to action</li><li><b>Contrast:</b> strategic contrasting colors direct attention to key elements</li><li><b>Spacing:</b> whitespace shows how elements relate</li></ul>`},
{h:'Accessibility and WCAG 2.2 AA (POUR)',b:`<p><b>WCAG 2.2 Level AA</b> is the legal standard in most jurisdictions, built around four principles: <b>P</b>erceivable, <b>O</b>perable, <b>U</b>nderstandable, <b>R</b>obust (<b>POUR</b>). Example app: <i>Be My Eyes</i>.</p><ul><li><b>Semantic HTML first:</b> use <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> not <code>&lt;div onclick&gt;</code>, plus <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code>. <b>ARIA</b> (<code>aria-label</code>, <code>aria-describedby</code>, <code>role="..."</code>) is a last resort when HTML falls short.</li><li><b>Color contrast:</b> at least <b>4.5:1</b> for body text and <b>3:1</b> for large text (Colour Contrast Analyser). Never rely on color alone; pair with text or icons.</li><li><b>Keyboard navigation:</b> every interactive element reachable and operable by keyboard alone, with visible focus indicators and logical tab order.</li><li><b>Alt text &amp; motor targets:</b> descriptive <code>alt</code> for informative images, <code>alt=""</code> for decorative images, long descriptions/tables for charts, and adequate touch target padding.</li></ul>`},
{h:'Cognitive load, contrast, proximity, alignment, dark patterns',b:`<ul><li><b>Cognitive load:</b> reduce mental effort; show only what is needed, group related items, never make users memorise. Use <b>progressive disclosure</b> (example: <i>Osisi</i> onboarding screen).</li><li><b>Contrast:</b> directs attention. Example: a red <b>"Delete account"</b> button on white commands attention and signals danger, while a grey <b>"Keep account"</b> button recedes to prevent accidental destructive clicks.</li><li><b>Proximity:</b> things that belong together stay together. On a video player, Play, Rewind and Fast-forward sit together, while Quit/Close sits apart to prevent accidental clicks.</li><li><b>Alignment:</b> clean lines and an invisible grid establish order, predictability and fast scanning.</li><li><b>Dark patterns:</b> deceptive UI/UX tricks that manipulate users into actions they did not intend.</li></ul>`},
{h:'The 5-step design process',b:`<table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Focus</th><th>What it produces</th></tr><tr><td><b>1. Research</b></td><td>Know the problem before designing anything</td><td>User interviews, competitive analysis, analytics insights</td></tr><tr><td><b>2. Define</b></td><td>Align on what success looks like</td><td>Personas, user stories, problem statements</td></tr><tr><td><b>3. Ideate</b></td><td>Quantity over quality; explore widely</td><td>Sketches, mood boards, low-fidelity wireframes</td></tr><tr><td><b>4. Prototype</b></td><td>Make it testable without building it</td><td>Clickable mockups in Figma or code</td></tr><tr><td><b>5. Test</b></td><td>Watch, don't explain; every insight is gold</td><td>Usability test recordings, findings, prioritised fixes</td></tr></table>`},
{h:'The frontend stack: skeleton, skin, muscles',b:`<table><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Metaphor</th><th>Key concepts on the slides</th></tr><tr><td><b>HTML</b></td><td>The skeleton (structure &amp; semantics)</td><td>Elements (<code>div, section, header, nav, main, footer</code>), semantic tags (SEO + accessibility), live DOM tree, forms/tables/media, attributes (<code>id, class, data-*, aria-*</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><b>CSS</b></td><td>The skin (presentation &amp; layout)</td><td>Cascade, specificity and inheritance; box model (content/padding/border/margin); <b>Flexbox</b> (1D: rows or columns); <b>Grid</b> (2D: rows and columns simultaneously); custom properties (<code>--tokens</code>); media &amp; container queries</td></tr><tr><td><b>JavaScript</b></td><td>The muscles (behaviour &amp; interactivity)</td><td>DOM manipulation &amp; events, Fetch API / async-await, ES Modules &amp; bundlers (Vite, Webpack), frameworks (React, Vue, Svelte, Solid), state management, TypeScript for type safety at scale</td></tr></table>`},
{h:'Component-based architecture & Atomic Design',b:`<p>Mental model: <b>Atomic Design</b> (Brad Frost). Core motto: <i>Build once. Use everywhere. Change in one place, update everything.</i></p><ul><li><b>Reusability:</b> write a component once, use it in 50 places</li><li><b>Encapsulation:</b> a component owns its own styles and logic</li><li><b>Single source of truth:</b> one definition, no duplication</li><li><b>Props → data in:</b> parent passes data down to children</li><li><b>Events → data out:</b> children communicate up via callbacks</li><li><b>Composition over inheritance:</b> combine small components rather than extending large ones</li></ul>`},
{h:'Design systems: tokens, component library, docs',b:`<p>A <b>design system</b> is the infrastructure layer that lets teams scale without chaos. It has three pillars:</p><ol><li><b>Design tokens:</b> named values for every design decision shared between design tools and code (<code>--color-primary: #3B82F6; --space-4: 1rem; --text-lg: 1.125rem; --radius-md: 0.375rem; --shadow-sm; --bp-tablet: 768px</code>).</li><li><b>Component library:</b> shared coded components (Button with default/hover/focus/disabled states, Input/Select/Checkbox/Radio, Modal/Drawer/Toast/Tooltip, Card/Badge/Avatar/Skeleton, Sidebar/Topnav/Breadcrumbs, Data tables + pagination).</li><li><b>Documentation:</b> hosted in <b>Storybook</b> or <b>Zeroheight</b> with usage guidelines, Do/Don't visual examples, accessibility notes, copy/tone guidelines and pattern templates. Skipping docs leads to drift within months.</li></ol>`}],
recall:[['UI vs UX?','UI is the visual interface; UX is the overall experience of using the product.'],['Role of HTML, CSS and JS.','Structure, presentation, behaviour.'],['What is responsive design?','Layouts adapt to screen size, using fluid grids and media queries.'],['Name three WCAG practices.','Sufficient contrast, alt text, keyboard navigation.'],['Visibility vs feedback in UI/UX?','Visibility is passive (system state is always readable at a glance, e.g. a cart badge); feedback is reactive (a direct response to a user action, e.g. spinner or error message).'],['Three visual cues used to establish hierarchy.','Font size and weight, contrast, and spacing.'],['What does POUR stand for in WCAG 2.2 AA?','Perceivable, Operable, Understandable, Robust.'],['WCAG AA minimum contrast ratios for body text and large text?','4.5:1 for body text and 3:1 for large text.'],['What attribute value should decorative images use for alt text?','An empty alt attribute: alt="".'],['Why should you use <button> instead of <div onclick>?','Semantic HTML gives screen readers and keyboards built-in accessibility; ARIA is only a last resort.'],['What technique reduces cognitive load on the Osisi onboarding screen?','Progressive disclosure (showing only what is needed at each step).'],['How does the video player example illustrate proximity?','Play, Rewind and Fast-forward are grouped together, while Quit/Close is placed separately to avoid accidental clicks.'],['Name the five steps of the UI/UX design process in order.','Research, Define, Ideate, Prototype, Test.'],['What are the metaphors for HTML, CSS and JavaScript on the slides?','HTML is the skeleton, CSS is the skin, JavaScript is the muscles.'],['Flexbox vs CSS Grid?','Flexbox is for 1D layout (rows or columns); Grid is for 2D layout (rows and columns simultaneously).'],['Who created Atomic Design, and what are the six component principles?','Brad Frost. Reusability, encapsulation, single source of truth, props (data in), events (data out), composition over inheritance.'],['Three parts of a design system and two documentation hosting tools.','Design tokens, component library, documentation (hosted in Storybook or Zeroheight).']],
quiz:[{k:'mcq',q:'Which CSS feature adapts styles to screen width?',o:['Media queries','Selectors','Variables','Animations'],a:0},{k:'tf',q:'A wireframe is a high-fidelity, fully interactive design.',a:false},{k:'gap',q:'React updates a virtual ___ before touching the real page.',a:'dom'},{k:'mcq',q:'A cart badge showing "3 items" before the user clicks anything illustrates…',o:['Feedback','Visibility','Proximity','Alignment'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'WCAG 2.2 Level AA requires what minimum contrast ratio for normal body text?',o:['2:1','3:1','4.5:1','7:1'],a:2},{k:'mcq',q:'In WCAG, POUR stands for Perceivable, Operable, Understandable and…',o:['Responsive','Robust','Reusable','Reactive'],a:1},{k:'tf',q:'ARIA attributes should always be used instead of semantic HTML elements like <button>.',a:false,w:'Correct semantic HTML is always the first step; ARIA is a last resort.'},{k:'gap',q:'Decorative images that carry no information should have alt="___" (leave empty or write empty).',a:'empty',alt:['""','']},{k:'mcq',q:'Revealing complex onboarding options step by step as needed is called…',o:['Dark patterns','Progressive disclosure','Atomic design','Specificity'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'Putting Play, Rewind and Fast-forward together while separating Quit/Close applies which principle?',o:['Proximity','Alignment','Consistency','Feedback'],a:0},{k:'mcq',q:'Which step of the design process produces personas, user stories and problem statements?',o:['Research','Define','Ideate','Prototype'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'In usability testing, the lecturer\'s rule of thumb is…',o:['Explain every button first','Watch, don\'t explain','Only test with developers','Skip low-fidelity wireframes'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'Which CSS layout system handles 2D rows and columns simultaneously?',o:['Flexbox','Box model','CSS Grid','Float'],a:2},{k:'gap',q:'Brad Frost\'s standard mental model for component hierarchy is ___ Design.',a:'atomic'},{k:'mcq',q:'In component architecture, how do child components send data up to parents?',o:['Props','Events / callbacks','Inheritance','CSS tokens'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'Named CSS custom properties like --color-primary: #3B82F6 are called design…',o:['Personas','Tokens','Frames','Queries'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'Which tools are named on the slides for hosting design system documentation?',o:['Docker and Kubernetes','Storybook and Zeroheight','Git and GitHub','Vite and Webpack'],a:1}],
theory:[{q:'Distinguish UI from UX and describe the UX design process for a campus food-ordering app.',pts:['UI def','UX def','Research/personas','Wireframes','Prototype','Usability testing','Iterate'],model:`<p>UI is the visual layer; UX is the whole experience. For a campus food app: interview students, create personas, map ordering flows, wireframe, build a clickable prototype, test with students and iterate on pain points such as checkout steps.</p>`},{q:'Explain the WCAG 2.2 AA accessibility standard, including the POUR principles and five concrete implementation rules from the lectures.',pts:['WCAG 2.2 AA legal standard','POUR: Perceivable, Operable, Understandable, Robust','Semantic HTML first, ARIA last resort','Contrast 4.5:1 body, 3:1 large text, not color alone','Keyboard navigation + visible focus + tab order','Alt text (informative vs decorative alt="") and motor touch targets'],model:`<p><b>Background:</b> accessibility means designing for the full spectrum of users, including people using screen readers, keyboard-only navigation or switch controls. WCAG 2.2 Level AA is the legal benchmark in most jurisdictions.</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b> WCAG is organised around four <b>POUR</b> principles: <b>Perceivable</b>, <b>Operable</b>, <b>Understandable</b> and <b>Robust</b>. Five lecture rules enforce it: (1) <b>Semantic HTML first</b> (use <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> so screen readers understand structure; use ARIA such as <code>aria-label</code> only where HTML falls short); (2) <b>Color contrast</b> of at least 4.5:1 for body text and 3:1 for large text, never relying on color alone; (3) <b>Keyboard navigation</b> with logical tab order and visible focus rings; (4) <b>Alt text</b> on all informative images, <code>alt=""</code> on decorative ones, and table alternatives for complex charts; (5) <b>Motor accessibility</b> via sufficient padding and touch target sizes.</p><p><b>Scenario:</b> on a campus health portal, replacing a clickable <code>&lt;div onclick&gt;</code> book button with a semantic <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> and boosting light-grey hint text to 4.5:1 contrast lets a visually impaired student book an appointment by keyboard and screen reader.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> semantic HTML and ARIA deliver Robust and Understandable structure, contrast and alt text make content Perceivable, and keyboard focus plus touch targets keep controls Operable.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> accessibility is non-negotiable engineering hygiene built from semantic HTML, measurable contrast, keyboard reachability and clear text alternatives.</p>`},{q:'Describe the three pillars of a Design System and the six principles of component-based architecture.',pts:['Design system purpose: scale without chaos','Pillar 1: Design tokens (--color-primary, --space-4)','Pillar 2: Component library (variants, states)','Pillar 3: Documentation (Storybook/Zeroheight, Do/Don\'t, a11y)','Six component principles: reusability, encapsulation, single source of truth, props down, events up, composition over inheritance','Atomic Design (Brad Frost)'],model:`<p><b>Background:</b> as frontend teams grow, ad-hoc styles cause visual drift, duplicated code and inconsistent behaviour. Component-based architecture (Brad Frost's Atomic Design) and Design Systems provide the shared infrastructure to scale without chaos.</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b> a component architecture follows six principles: <b>reusability</b> (build once, use in 50 places), <b>encapsulation</b> (owns its styles and logic), <b>single source of truth</b>, <b>props for data in</b> (parent to child), <b>events/callbacks for data out</b> (child to parent), and <b>composition over inheritance</b>. A Design System packages this into three pillars: (1) <b>Design tokens</b> (named values like <code>--color-primary: #3B82F6</code> and <code>--space-4: 1rem</code> shared between Figma and CSS); (2) a <b>Component library</b> covering every variant and state (default, hover, focus, disabled) for buttons, inputs, modals, cards and tables; (3) <b>Documentation</b> hosted in Storybook or Zeroheight with usage rules, Do/Don't examples, accessibility notes and tone guidelines.</p><p><b>Scenario:</b> in the Osisi family-tree web app, defining danger red and spacing as tokens and sharing a single <code>Button</code> component means updating the focus ring in one file fixes accessibility across every screen.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> tokens feed consistent values into encapsulated components, while Storybook documentation prevents teams from misusing or re-inventing them.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> without documentation and tokens, a component folder drifts within months; all three pillars are required for a true design system.</p>`}]},
{id:'t2',title:'Front-end practical',why:'Hands-on lab: expect "write the code" questions.',
cards:[
{h:'DOM and events',b:`<pre>const btn = document.querySelector('#add');
btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
  const li = document.createElement('li');
  li.textContent = input.value;
  list.appendChild(li);
});</pre><p>Fetch API: <code>const res = await fetch('/api/items'); const data = await res.json();</code></p>`},
{h:'React component',b:`<pre>function Counter(){
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  return &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setCount(count + 1)}&gt;{count}&lt;/button&gt;;
}</pre><p><b>Props</b> pass data down; <b>state</b> is local, changing state re-renders. <code>useEffect</code> runs side effects (fetching).</p>`},
{h:'Audiobook player component tree',b:`<p>In the lecturer's Frontend Practical tutorial, a React UI is a <b>tree of functions (components)</b>: each function takes data in and returns what the screen should look like. When data changes, React re-runs the function and updates only what changed.</p><pre>PlayerScreen          &lt;- root component
├── PlayerHeader      &lt;- "NOW PLAYING", title, chapter picker
├── CoverArt          &lt;- image, "UNABRIDGED" badge
├── TrackInfo         &lt;- title, author, narrator
├── ProgressBar       &lt;- 12:34 / 35:00 / -22:26
├── Controls          &lt;- -15s, bookmark, play/pause, +30s, 1.0x
└── BookmarkList      &lt;- list of rows
    └── BookmarkRow   &lt;- ▶ 12:34 "Holmes picks up the letter" 🗑</pre>`},
{h:'Components, JSX, props and state',b:`<ul><li><b>Component &amp; JSX:</b> a function starting with a <b>capital letter</b> that returns JSX (HTML-like syntax in JS). Curly braces <code>{}</code> embed any JS expression.</li><li><b>Props:</b> read-only inputs that flow <b>down</b> from parent to child (e.g. <code>title, author, narrator</code> into <code>TrackInfo</code>).</li><li><b>State:</b> data a component owns that changes over time. <code>const [isPlaying, setIsPlaying] = useState(true);</code> returns <code>[currentValue, setterFunction]</code>.</li></ul><div class="trap">Never mutate state directly (<code>isPlaying = false</code> does nothing) and never call a handler during render: pass a function to <code>onClick={() =&gt; setIsPlaying(!isPlaying)}</code>.</div>`},
{h:'Conditional rendering, lists and stable keys',b:`<pre>function BookmarkNote({ note }: { note: string }) {
  return note ? &lt;span&gt;"{note}"&lt;/span&gt; : &lt;em&gt;(no note)&lt;/em&gt;;
}
// Show only when true:
{isPlaying &amp;&amp; &lt;SpeakerIcon /&gt;}</pre><p><b>Lists and keys:</b> render arrays with <code>.map()</code> and give each item a stable, unique <code>key={b.id}</code>.</p><div class="trap">Always use a real ID (like <code>b.id</code> or <code>crypto.randomUUID()</code>) for <code>key</code>. Using the array index causes bugs when items are deleted or reordered.</div>`},
{h:'Lifting state up, callbacks and immutable updates',b:`<p>When both the play button and the progress bar depend on <code>isPlaying</code>, neither child can own it. <b>Lift state up</b> to their closest shared parent (<code>PlayerScreen</code>) and pass it down via props; children talk back to parents via <b>callback props</b> (<code>onTogglePlay</code>, <code>onSkip</code>, <code>onBookmark</code>, <code>onDelete</code>).</p><pre>const addBookmark = () =&gt; {
  setBookmarks((prev) =&gt; [
    ...prev,
    { id: crypto.randomUUID(), time: formatTime(position), note: '' },
  ]);
};
const deleteBookmark = (id: string) =&gt; {
  setBookmarks((prev) =&gt; prev.filter((b) =&gt; b.id !== id));
};</pre><p>When new state depends on old state, pass an updater function <code>(prev) =&gt; ...</code> and return a <b>new array</b> (<code>[...prev, x]</code>, <code>.filter()</code>). Never <code>.push()</code> into state.</p>`},
{h:'Effects, custom hooks and derived values',b:`<pre>function usePlaybackTimer(isPlaying: boolean, onTick: () =&gt; void) {
  useEffect(() =&gt; {
    if (!isPlaying) return;
    const id = setInterval(onTick, 1000);
    return () =&gt; clearInterval(id); // cleanup on unmount or re-run
  }, [isPlaying, onTick]); // dependency array
}</pre><ul><li><b>Effects (<code>useEffect</code>):</b> for syncing with the outside world (timers, network, audio, subscriptions), <b>not</b> for values you can compute during render. Always clean up timers/listeners; forgetting a dependency causes stale-data bugs.</li><li><b>Custom hook:</b> a function starting with <code>use</code> (like <code>usePlaybackTimer</code>) that bundles reusable stateful logic.</li><li><b>Derived values:</b> don't store what you can compute! <code>const remaining = total - position; const percent = (position / total) * 100;</code> Duplicated state goes out of sync; computed values cannot.</li></ul>`},
{h:'Why Zustand exists: solving prop drilling',b:`<p>Passing <code>isPlaying</code>, <code>position</code> and callbacks through five layers of components (mini-player, lock-screen controls, library screen) is called <b>prop drilling</b>. The fix is moving shared client state into a <b>Zustand store</b> outside the component tree:</p><pre>function PlayPauseButton() {
  const isPlaying = usePlayerStore((s) =&gt; s.isPlaying);
  const toggle = usePlayerStore((s) =&gt; s.togglePlay);
  return &lt;button onClick={toggle}&gt;{isPlaying ? '⏸' : '▶'}&lt;/button&gt;;
}</pre><p><b>Practical rule:</b> state used by one component or a parent and its direct children → <code>useState</code>. State shared across distant parts of the app (playback, user settings) → a Zustand store.</p>`}],
recall:[['How do you attach a click handler in plain JS?','element.addEventListener("click", handler).'],['Props vs state?','Props are passed in from the parent and read-only; state is local and changing it re-renders.'],['Which hook fetches data after render?','useEffect.'],['Why must React component names start with a capital letter?','So JSX treats them as React components rather than built-in HTML tags.'],['What happens if you use an array index as a React list key when items can be deleted?','React mismatches component state and DOM nodes when items are deleted or reordered; always use a stable unique ID.'],['Two rules of data and event flow in a React component tree.','Data flows down through props; events flow up through callback props.'],['Why should you never call .push() on a React state array?','It mutates the existing array in place so React does not see a new reference and will not re-render; create a new array with [...prev, item] or .filter().'],['What are the three rules of thumb for useEffect on the slides?','Use effects only for syncing with external systems (not derived values), always clean up timers/listeners, and include all dependencies to avoid stale-data bugs.'],['Why shouldn\'t remaining playback time (total - position) be stored in useState?','It is a derived value; storing it duplicates state that can go out of sync, whereas computing it during render is always exact.'],['What is a custom hook in React?','A function whose name starts with "use" (such as usePlaybackTimer) that bundles reusable hook logic.'],['What is prop drilling, and how does Zustand fix it?','Threading props through many intermediate layers; Zustand moves shared state into an external store so any component can subscribe directly with usePlayerStore(s => ...).'],['When should you choose useState vs a Zustand store?','useState for state local to one component or a parent and its direct children; Zustand for state shared across distant screens.']],
quiz:[{k:'mcq',q:'Which method parses a fetch response body as JSON?',o:['res.text()','res.json()','JSON.stringify()','res.body()'],a:1},{k:'gap',q:'In React, useState returns the value and a ___ function.',a:'setter',alt:['set','update']},{k:'mcq',q:'Why does isPlaying = false fail to update a React screen?',o:['booleans cannot be rendered','Direct mutation does not trigger a React re-render; you must call the state setter','useState only accepts numbers','isPlaying is a reserved keyword'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'Which JSX pattern renders <SpeakerIcon /> only when isPlaying is true?',o:['{isPlaying || <SpeakerIcon />}','{isPlaying && <SpeakerIcon />}','{isPlaying ? null : <SpeakerIcon />}','<SpeakerIcon if={isPlaying} />'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'When rendering bookmarks with .map(), what should you pass to the key prop?',o:['The array index i','Math.random()','A stable unique ID such as b.id','The bookmark note text'],a:2},{k:'tf',q:'To remove an item from a React state array, you should use prev.filter(...) inside the setter.',a:true},{k:'mcq',q:'In usePlaybackTimer, what does the function returned from useEffect do?',o:['Starts the timer immediately','Cleans up the interval with clearInterval(id) before re-running or on unmount','Updates the DOM directly','Replaces useState'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'In the audiobook player, remaining = total - position should be stored in…',o:['useState','useEffect','A plain const computed during render (derived value)','localStorage'],a:2},{k:'gap',q:'Passing props through five layers of components that do not use them is called prop ___.',a:'drilling'},{k:'mcq',q:'A reusable React function starting with "use" (like usePlaybackTimer) is a…',o:['Higher-order component','Custom hook','Reducer','Portal'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'In the Frontend Practical, which library holds shared client state outside components to avoid prop drilling?',o:['Redux Saga','Zustand','Express','Prisma'],a:1}],
theory:[{q:'Write a React component that fetches a list of courses from /api/courses and displays them.',pts:['useState for list','useEffect with fetch','await res.json()','map to li with key','Loading/error handling'],model:`<pre>function Courses(){
  const [items,setItems]=useState([]);
  useEffect(()=>{ fetch("/api/courses").then(r=>r.json()).then(setItems); },[]);
  return &lt;ul&gt;{items.map(c=>&lt;li key={c.id}&gt;{c.title}&lt;/li&gt;)}&lt;/ul&gt;;
}</pre>`},{q:'Using the audiobook player from the Frontend Practical, explain lifting state up, derived values, useEffect cleanup and when to move state from useState to Zustand.',pts:['Lifting state up to PlayerScreen + props down, callbacks up','Immutable state updates ([...prev, item], .filter)','Derived values: compute remaining = total - position during render','useEffect + setInterval + return () => clearInterval(id) cleanup','Prop drilling problem across distant screens','Zustand store (usePlayerStore) vs local useState rule'],model:`<p><b>Background:</b> in React, the UI is a tree of component functions. Managing where state lives determines whether an app stays clean or breaks with stale data and prop drilling.</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b> (1) <b>Lifting state up:</b> because both <code>ProgressBar</code> and <code>Controls</code> need <code>position</code> and <code>isPlaying</code>, state is lifted to their shared parent <code>PlayerScreen</code>, passed down as props, and updated upward via callbacks like <code>onTogglePlay</code> and immutable array updaters (<code>prev.filter</code>, <code>[...prev, newItem]</code>). (2) <b>Derived values:</b> remaining time (<code>total - position</code>) and progress percentage are computed directly during render rather than stored in <code>useState</code>, because duplicated state can drift out of sync. (3) <b>Effects and cleanup:</b> the 1-second playback timer is an external side effect wrapped in a custom hook <code>usePlaybackTimer</code>; <code>useEffect</code> starts <code>setInterval</code> and returns <code>() =&gt; clearInterval(id)</code> so timers do not leak on unmount or pause. (4) <b>Zustand vs useState:</b> when distant screens (mini-player, lock-screen, library) all need playback state, passing props through five layers causes prop drilling; moving <code>isPlaying</code> and <code>position</code> into <code>usePlayerStore</code> lets each component select only what it needs.</p><p><b>Scenario:</b> when a listener taps "+30s" in <code>Controls</code>, the callback updates <code>position</code> in the store, <code>ProgressBar</code> automatically recalculates <code>remaining</code> on render, and pausing triggers the effect cleanup to stop the interval.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> local state and derived values keep individual components simple, effects safely bridge external timers, and Zustand shares cross-screen client state without prop drilling.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> compute what you can, keep local state in <code>useState</code>, clean up every effect, and promote shared UI state to Zustand.</p>`}]},
{id:'t3',title:'Backend development',why:'Client–server, REST, HTTP methods and status codes, databases, auth.',
cards:[
{h:'Client–server and REST',b:`<p>The backend handles business logic, data storage, authentication and APIs. <b>REST</b>: resources at URLs, stateless requests, standard HTTP methods.</p><table><tr><th>Method</th><th>Use</th></tr><tr><td>GET</td><td>Read</td></tr><tr><td>POST</td><td>Create</td></tr><tr><td>PUT/PATCH</td><td>Replace/update</td></tr><tr><td>DELETE</td><td>Remove</td></tr></table><p>Status codes: 200 OK · 201 Created · 400 Bad Request · 401 Unauthorized · 403 Forbidden · 404 Not Found · 500 Server Error.</p>`},
{h:'Stack pieces',b:`<p>Runtimes/frameworks: Node.js + Express, Spring Boot, Django, Laravel. Databases: SQL (PostgreSQL, MySQL) vs NoSQL (MongoDB). <b>ORM</b> maps tables to objects. Auth: sessions vs <b>JWT</b> tokens; hash passwords (bcrypt). Middleware: functions that run on each request (logging, auth).</p>`},
{h:'Why backend matters & five core concepts',b:`<p>The backend is more than "just a server": it is the logic, data and infrastructure that powers functionality, maintains integrity and enables scale (offline/online sync, real-time collaboration, versioning). Its three pillars are <b>Data Management</b>, <b>Business Logic</b> and <b>Security &amp; Authentication</b>.</p><p><b>Five core backend concepts on the slides:</b></p><ol><li><b>Data Modeling</b></li><li><b>APIs (Application Programming Interfaces)</b></li><li><b>Business Logic</b></li><li><b>Authentication &amp; Authorization</b></li><li><b>Data Validation</b></li></ol>`},
{h:'Data modeling (osisi) & schema design principles',b:`<p><b>osisi core entities:</b> 1. Users · 2. Families · 3. Family Members · 4. Relationships · 5. Messaging. Rule: <i>One user owns many families. One family has many members. Members are connected via relationships.</i></p><table><tr><th>Schema principle</th><th>Rule &amp; osisi example</th></tr><tr><td><b>1. Normalization</b></td><td>Avoid data duplication; store each piece of data once. Example: a family member is stored once and referenced across multiple relationships.</td></tr><tr><td><b>2. Relationships &amp; Indexes</b></td><td>Use foreign keys and indexes for fast queries. Example: index on <code>family_id</code> to quickly find all members of a family.</td></tr><tr><td><b>3. Validation</b></td><td>Define data types and constraints at the schema level (valid email format, max name length).</td></tr></table>`},
{h:'APIs and business logic',b:`<p><b>APIs: the contract between frontend and backend.</b> Every API specifies three things:</p><ul><li><b>Endpoints:</b> functions clients can call (<code>createFamily()</code>, <code>getFamilyMembers()</code>, <code>updateRelationship()</code>)</li><li><b>Input parameters:</b> data the function needs (<code>createFamily</code> needs <code>name, owner_id, is_public</code>)</li><li><b>Return values:</b> data returned (<code>getFamilyMembers</code> returns an array of member objects)</li></ul><p><b>Four kinds of business logic on the slides:</b> (1) <b>Complex calculations</b> (match algorithm comparing members across trees) · (2) <b>Data integrity rules</b> (a person cannot be their own parent; partnership requires two distinct members) · (3) <b>Cascading operations</b> (deleting a family member removes all their relationships) · (4) <b>Business workflows</b> (collaboration request: send notification → await approval → grant access).</p>`},
{h:'Authentication vs authorization & the 4 osisi roles',b:`<p><b>Authentication</b> verifies <i>who the user is</i> (email/password or OAuth with Google/Facebook → backend issues a session token). <b>Authorization</b> determines <i>what the user can do</i>, checked before every operation.</p><table><tr><th>osisi Role</th><th>Access level</th><th>Permissions</th></tr><tr><td><b>1. Owner</b></td><td>All access</td><td>Full control: create, read, update, delete</td></tr><tr><td><b>2. Editor</b></td><td>Write access</td><td>Can add and edit family members and relationships</td></tr><tr><td><b>3. Viewer</b></td><td>Read only</td><td>Can only view the family tree, no modifications</td></tr><tr><td><b>4. Limited</b></td><td>Partial access</td><td>Restricted view, certain members hidden</td></tr></table>`},
{h:'4-level validation, query optimization & scalability',b:`<table><tr><th>4 Validation levels</th><th>3 Query optimizations</th><th>4 Scalability techniques</th></tr><tr><td><b>1. Type:</b> string, number, boolean, date</td><td><b>1. Indexes:</b> speed up searches on frequently queried fields</td><td><b>1. Caching:</b> keep hot data in memory</td></tr><tr><td><b>2. Format:</b> email format, phone patterns</td><td><b>2. Selective queries:</b> fetch only the fields you need</td><td><b>2. DB optimization:</b> indexes + query design</td></tr><tr><td><b>3. Business rules:</b> domain constraints</td><td><b>3. Pagination:</b> load data in chunks, not all at once</td><td><b>3. Async operations:</b> don't block user requests on long tasks</td></tr><tr><td><b>4. Existence:</b> verify referenced entities exist</td><td></td><td><b>4. Resource limits:</b> rate limiting and usage quotas</td></tr></table>`},
{h:'Error handling (4 categories) & backend testing (3 types)',b:`<p><b>Four error categories with slide examples:</b></p><ul><li><b>1. Validation errors:</b> <i>"Family name must be between 2-100 characters"</i></li><li><b>2. Authorization errors:</b> <i>"You don't have permission to delete this family"</i></li><li><b>3. Server errors:</b> log details internally for debugging, show a friendly message: <i>"An unexpected server error occurred. Please try again later"</i></li><li><b>4. Not found errors:</b> <i>"Family not found or has been deleted"</i></li></ul><p><b>Three backend test levels:</b> (1) <b>Unit tests</b> (one function in isolation, e.g. <code>createFamilyMember</code> name length) · (2) <b>Integration tests</b> (multiple components together: create member → add relationship → query descendants) · (3) <b>Edge cases</b> (circular relationships, empty families).</p>`}],
recall:[['Name the four main HTTP methods and their uses.','GET read, POST create, PUT/PATCH update, DELETE remove.'],['401 vs 403?','401: not authenticated; 403: authenticated but not allowed.'],['What does "stateless" mean in REST?','Each request carries all information needed; the server stores no client session state between requests.'],['What is an ORM?','A layer that maps database tables to programming-language objects.'],['Name the five core backend concepts on the slides.','Data Modeling, APIs, Business Logic, Authentication & Authorization, and Data Validation.'],['What are the five core entities in the osisi data model?','Users, Families, Family Members, Relationships, and Messaging.'],['Three schema design principles from the Backend lecture.','Normalization (store each piece of data once), Relationships & Indexes (foreign keys and indexes like family_id), and Validation (types and constraints at schema level).'],['What three things does an API contract specify?','Endpoints (functions to call), Input Parameters (what data it needs), and Return Values (what data it returns).'],['Name the four types of business logic with an osisi example.','Complex calculations (tree matching), Data integrity rules (cannot be own parent), Cascading operations (deleting a member deletes relationships), Business workflows (invite -> approve -> grant access).'],['Name the four authorization roles in osisi and what each can do.','Owner (full CRUD), Editor (add/edit members and relationships), Viewer (read-only tree), Limited (restricted view with certain members hidden).'],['What are the four levels of data validation on the slides?','Type validation, Format validation, Business rules validation, and Existence validation.'],['Three query optimization techniques from the Backend slides.','Indexes on frequently queried fields, Selective queries (fetch only needed data), and Pagination (load in chunks).'],['Four scalability considerations for a growing backend.','Caching in memory, Database optimization, Async operations for long tasks, and Resource limits (rate limiting and usage quotas).'],['Name the four backend error categories and how server errors should be handled.','Validation, Authorization, Server, and Not Found errors. Server errors must be logged internally while showing a user-friendly message to the client.'],['Three levels of backend testing on the slides.','Unit tests (functions in isolation), Integration tests (components working together), and Edge cases (circular relationships, empty families).']],
quiz:[{k:'mcq',q:'Creating a new user returns which status code?',o:['200','201','204','404'],a:1},{k:'tf',q:'Passwords should be stored encrypted so they can be decrypted later.',a:false,w:'They should be hashed (bcrypt), not reversibly encrypted.'},{k:'gap',q:'JWT stands for JSON Web ___.',a:'token'},{k:'mcq',q:'In the osisi schema, storing each family member once and referencing them across relationships is…',o:['Pagination','Normalization','Caching','Cascading'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'Which osisi role gives a collaborator a restricted view with certain family members hidden?',o:['Owner','Editor','Viewer','Limited'],a:3},{k:'mcq',q:'"A person cannot be their own parent" and "deleting a member removes all their relationships" belong to…',o:['Business logic (data integrity and cascading operations)','UI hierarchy','Frontend routing','Static asset caching'],a:0},{k:'mcq',q:'Checking that a referenced familyId or productId actually points to a real row in the database is…',o:['Type validation','Format validation','Existence validation','CSS validation'],a:2},{k:'mcq',q:'Loading large query results in chunks rather than all at once is called…',o:['Normalization','Pagination','Cascading','Hashing'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'How should a backend handle an unexpected 500 server error?',o:['Send the full stack trace and database password to the browser','Log the error internally for debugging and return a user-friendly message','Crash the server silently with no response','Delete the user session'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'Testing "create member -> add relationship -> query descendants" together is an example of…',o:['Unit testing','Integration testing','Static typing','Visual regression'],a:1},{k:'gap',q:'Preventing API abuse by capping requests per user or IP is called ___ limiting.',a:'rate'}],
theory:[{q:'Design a REST API for a library\'s books resource, with methods, endpoints and status codes.',pts:['GET /books','GET /books/:id','POST /books → 201','PUT/PATCH /books/:id','DELETE /books/:id','Error codes 400/404','Auth'],model:`<p>GET /books (200), GET /books/{id} (200 or 404), POST /books (201, 400 on invalid data), PUT /books/{id} (200), DELETE /books/{id} (204). Protect write routes with JWT auth (401/403).</p>`},{q:'Using the osisi family-tree application from the lectures, explain the five core backend concepts, the four authorization roles, and the four levels of data validation.',pts:['5 core concepts: data modeling, APIs, business logic, auth/authz, data validation','osisi entities (Users, Families, Family Members, Relationships, Messaging) + schema principles','APIs as contract (endpoints, input params, return values) + business logic (integrity, cascading, workflows)','Authentication vs Authorization + 4 osisi roles (Owner, Editor, Viewer, Limited)','4 validation levels: type, format, business rules, existence'],model:`<p><b>Background:</b> the backend is the server-side brain of an application, responsible for data management, business rules and security. In the lectures, these concepts are illustrated through <i>osisi</i>, a sensitive family-tree platform.</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b> osisi is built around five core backend concepts: (1) <b>Data modeling</b> across five entities (Users, Families, Family Members, Relationships, Messaging) using normalization, foreign-key references and indexes such as <code>family_id</code>; (2) <b>APIs</b> as a contract defining endpoints (<code>createFamily</code>, <code>getFamilyMembers</code>), input parameters and return values; (3) <b>Business logic</b> enforcing data integrity (a person cannot be their own parent), cascading deletes (removing a member deletes their relationships) and approval workflows; (4) <b>Authentication and authorization</b>, where authentication verifies identity (email/password or OAuth token) and authorization enforces four roles: <b>Owner</b> (full CRUD), <b>Editor</b> (add/edit members and relationships), <b>Viewer</b> (read-only) and <b>Limited</b> (restricted view with hidden members); (5) <b>Data validation</b> across four levels: <b>Type</b> (string, number, boolean), <b>Format</b> (valid email/phone), <b>Business rules</b> (domain constraints) and <b>Existence</b> (verifying referenced IDs exist).</p><p><b>Scenario:</b> when a user tries to add a parent relationship in osisi, the backend authenticates their session token, checks that their role is Owner or Editor, validates parameter types and formats, verifies both member IDs exist, and confirms the member is not being set as their own parent.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> schema indexes and normalization keep storage clean, while auth checks, four-layer validation and business logic guard every API endpoint before any write touches the database.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> a production backend never trusts client calls; it combines normalized schemas, role-based authorization, multi-layer validation and explicit error handling on every request.</p>`}]},
{id:'t4',title:'Backend practical',why:'Expect to write a route handler.',
cards:[
{h:'Express route (additional context)',b:`<div class="trap"><b>Additional context:</b> the lecturer's Backend Practical teaches <b>Convex</b> (TypeScript queries, mutations and actions), not Express. The cards below cover the Convex backend from the slides and the Feb 2026 past exam.</div><pre>const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());
app.get('/api/books', async (req, res) => {
  const books = await Book.find();
  res.json(books);
});
app.post('/api/books', async (req, res) => {
  const book = await Book.create(req.body);
  res.status(201).json(book);
});
app.listen(3000);</pre><p><code>express.json()</code> parses JSON bodies; <code>req.params</code> for path values; <code>req.query</code> for ?query strings.</p>`},
{h:'What is Convex? Convex vs Zustand',b:`<p><b>Convex</b> is a backend platform with a database, TypeScript server functions and automatic real-time sync built in. You write server functions in TypeScript and call them from React; when underlying data changes, the UI updates automatically with no REST endpoints, manual cache invalidation or WebSocket boilerplate.</p><div class="def"><b>Convex vs Zustand (in one line)</b><b>Zustand</b> holds fast local <b>client state</b> (is audio playing, current 1-second playback tick, which panel is open). <b>Convex</b> holds durable <b>server state</b> (the book catalog, saved playback progress, bookmarks). Use both; never put server data in Zustand or sync to the DB on every tick.</div>`},
{h:'Convex setup & schema definition (audiobook tutorial)',b:`<p>Root setup uses <code>VITE_CONVEX_URL</code> in Vite or <code>NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL</code> in Next.js with <code>&lt;ConvexProvider client={convex}&gt;</code>. Every document automatically gets <code>_id</code> and <code>_creationTime</code>.</p><pre>// convex/schema.ts
import { defineSchema, defineTable } from 'convex/server';
import { v } from 'convex/values';
export default defineSchema({
  books: defineTable({
    title: v.string(), author: v.string(), narrator: v.string(),
    isUnabridged: v.boolean(), durationSeconds: v.number(),
    coverStorageId: v.optional(v.id('_storage')),
    chapters: v.array(v.object({ title: v.string(), startTime: v.number() })),
  }),
  playbackProgress: defineTable({
    userId: v.string(), bookId: v.id('books'),
    position: v.number(), speed: v.number(), updatedAt: v.number(),
  }).index('by_user_book', ['userId', 'bookId']),
  bookmarks: defineTable({
    userId: v.string(), bookId: v.id('books'),
    chapter: v.number(), time: v.number(), note: v.optional(v.string()),
  }).index('by_user_book_chapter_time', ['userId', 'bookId', 'chapter', 'time']),
});</pre><p><b>Three schema design decisions:</b> (1) Use typed relations <code>v.id('books')</code> instead of <code>v.string()</code>; (2) <b>Embed</b> small, always-together data like <code>chapters</code> inside <code>books</code> rather than creating a fourth table; (3) Index <code>['userId','bookId','chapter','time']</code> returns bookmarks auto-sorted by <code>time</code> without a full table scan.</p>`},
{h:'Three Convex server function types & internalMutation',b:`<table><tr><th>Function type</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Can write to DB?</th><th>Can call external APIs (fetch)?</th></tr><tr><td><b>query</b></td><td>Read data. Reactive, cached, live.</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><b>mutation</b></td><td>Write data. Runs as an atomic transaction.</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><b>action</b></td><td>Call third-party services (payments, AI, email).</td><td>Via mutations</td><td>Yes</td></tr></table><p><b><code>internalMutation</code>:</b> cannot be called from the browser, only from other server code or the CLI (<code>npx convex run books:seed</code>). Use it for seeding and admin operations users must never trigger.</p>`},
{h:'Queries, upsert mutations, TypeScript joins & untrusted client rule',b:`<p><b>Non-negotiable rule: the client is untrusted.</b> Always check <code>const identity = await ctx.auth.getUserIdentity();</code>, verify referenced rows exist (<code>ctx.db.get</code>), clamp numeric inputs, enforce caps inside the mutation (e.g. max 10 bookmarks per chapter, 80-char note slice), and throw <code>ConvexError</code> for user-facing errors.</p><pre>// Indexed unique lookup + Upsert (patch if existing, insert if not)
const existing = await ctx.db.query('playbackProgress')
  .withIndex('by_user_book', (q) =&gt; q.eq('userId', userId).eq('bookId', args.bookId))
  .unique();
if (existing) await ctx.db.patch(existing._id, data);
else await ctx.db.insert('playbackProgress', { userId, bookId: args.bookId, ...data });</pre><p><b>Joining tables in Convex:</b> there is no SQL JOIN. You fetch records and join in TypeScript with <code>Promise.all(books.map(async (book) =&gt; ...))</code>. Resolve file URLs with <code>ctx.storage.getUrl(book.coverStorageId)</code>.</p>`},
{h:'React hooks, throttled sync & optimistic updates',b:`<ul><li><b><code>useQuery(api.books.get, { id })</code>:</b> returns an unset value while loading, <code>null</code> if not found, or the live data.</li><li><b><code>useMutation(api.bookmarks.add)</code>:</b> returns a function to trigger the mutation; catch <code>err instanceof ConvexError</code> and read <code>err.data</code>.</li><li><b>Throttled progress sync (<code>useSyncProgress</code>):</b> don't write playback position every second! Read <code>usePlayerStore.getState()</code> (without re-rendering) and save to Convex every <b>20 seconds</b> while playing and once on pause.</li><li><b>Optimistic updates:</b> chain <code>.withOptimisticUpdate((localStore, { id }) =&gt; ...)</code> on <code>useMutation</code> to update the local query cache immediately; Convex rolls it back automatically if the server mutation fails.</li></ul>`},
{h:'Convex Do vs Don\'t rules (exam code-review checklist)',b:`<table><tr><th>Do (Best Practice)</th><th>Don't (Exam Code-Review Flaw)</th></tr><tr><td>Query through indexes (<code>.withIndex(...)</code>)</td><td>Use <code>.filter()</code> over a whole table (full table scan that slows down as data grows)</td></tr><tr><td>Use <code>v.id('table')</code> for references between tables</td><td>Use <code>v.string()</code> for foreign IDs (loses type safety and table validation)</td></tr><tr><td>Embed small, always-together data (chapters)</td><td>Create a separate table for everything, or cram unrelated role fields into one giant table</td></tr><tr><td>Use an index + <code>.unique()</code> for one-row-per-user records</td><td>Use <code>.filter(...).first()</code> without an index or uniqueness guarantee</td></tr><tr><td>Check <code>ctx.auth.getUserIdentity()</code>, validate &amp; clamp args in every function</td><td>Trust anything sent from the client (missing auth, negative stock, unvalidated enums)</td></tr><tr><td>Throw <code>ConvexError</code> for user-facing errors</td><td>Throw plain <code>Error</code> or return <code>{ success: true }</code> silently</td></tr><tr><td>Use <code>internalMutation</code> for admin/seed tasks and <code>action</code> for external <code>fetch</code></td><td>Expose admin mutations publicly or call external APIs inside queries/mutations</td></tr></table>`}],
recall:[['Which middleware parses JSON bodies in Express?','express.json().'],['req.params vs req.query?','params: path segments (/books/:id); query: ?key=value.'],['How do Convex and Zustand divide state in the audiobook tutorial?','Zustand holds fast client UI state (isPlaying, 1-second position ticks, open panels); Convex holds durable server state (books catalog, saved playbackProgress, bookmarks).'],['Which two fields does Convex add automatically to every document?','_id and _creationTime.'],['Name the three Convex server function types and which ones can write to the database or call external APIs.','query (read-only, reactive, no DB writes, no external APIs), mutation (transactional DB writes, no external APIs), action (can call external APIs and writes to DB via mutations).'],['What is an internalMutation in Convex, and how do you run one from the terminal?','A server mutation that cannot be called from the browser/client, only from other server functions or the CLI via npx convex run <file>:<fn> (e.g. npx convex run books:seed).'],['Why should you use v.id("books") instead of v.string() for foreign keys in Convex?','v.id("books") enforces that the ID refers to an actual document in the books table and provides end-to-end TypeScript type safety.'],['Why is .withIndex() preferred over .filter() in Convex queries?','.withIndex() uses a database index for fast lookup and sorting, whereas .filter() scans the entire table and becomes slow as data grows.'],['In React, what is the difference between useQuery while still loading vs when a record does not exist?','While loading, useQuery has not returned a document yet (loading state); once loaded, it returns null if the document does not exist (or the user is not signed in).'],['How does Convex perform table joins since it has no SQL JOIN keyword?','By querying the related records in TypeScript inside the query handler (for example using Promise.all over an array of documents).'],['How does useSyncProgress avoid hammering the Convex database every second during audio playback?','It keeps the 1-second position in Zustand, reads usePlayerStore.getState() without subscribing, and saves to Convex only every 20 seconds while playing and once on pause.'],['What does .withOptimisticUpdate() do on a Convex mutation?','It updates the local query cache in the browser immediately so the UI feels instant, and automatically rolls back if the server mutation fails.']],
quiz:[{k:'mcq',q:'In /books/42, how do you read 42 with route /books/:id?',o:['req.query.id','req.params.id','req.body.id','req.id'],a:1},{k:'tf',q:'res.status(201).json(obj) sends a Created response with JSON.',a:true},{k:'mcq',q:'Which Convex function type is allowed to call third-party REST APIs such as Paystack, OpenAI or Resend?',o:['query','mutation','action','internalQuery'],a:2},{k:'mcq',q:'Which Convex function type runs as an atomic database transaction to insert, patch or delete data?',o:['query','mutation','action','useEffect'],a:1},{k:'tf',q:'In Convex, a query function can call external fetch() APIs directly.',a:false,w:'Queries are deterministic and read-only; external API calls must go in an action.'},{k:'mcq',q:'To prevent browser clients from calling a database seed function directly, declare it as…',o:['query','mutation','internalMutation','publicAction'],a:2},{k:'mcq',q:'In a Convex schema, which validator creates a type-safe reference to a document in the users table?',o:['v.string("users")','v.id("users")','v.ref("users")','v.number()'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'Why is ctx.db.query("inventory").filter(...).first() a flaw in a production Convex mutation?',o:['Convex does not support .first()','.filter() scans the entire table instead of using .withIndex(), causing poor performance at scale','mutations cannot read the database','filter only works on strings'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'When using Convex useQuery in React, what return value indicates the query finished loading and the document was not found?',o:['null','false','0','""'],a:0},{k:'gap',q:'For user-facing error messages in Convex mutations, throw new ___("Message") instead of a plain Error.',a:'convexerror',alt:['ConvexError']},{k:'mcq',q:'How does the audiobook tutorial read Zustand state inside setInterval without causing React re-renders every second?',o:['usePlayerStore.getState()','useState(0)','ctx.db.get()','localStorage.getItem()'],a:0},{k:'tf',q:'Enforcing a 10-bookmark limit in the React UI is sufficient; the Convex mutation does not need to check the limit.',a:false,w:'The client is untrusted; business rules must always be enforced inside the server mutation.'}],
theory:[{q:'Write Express routes to get one book by id and delete it, handling not-found.',pts:['GET /:id','404 if missing','DELETE','204','async/await'],model:`<pre>app.get("/api/books/:id", async (req,res)=>{
  const b = await Book.findById(req.params.id);
  if(!b) return res.status(404).json({error:"Not found"});
  res.json(b);
});
app.delete("/api/books/:id", async (req,res)=>{
  await Book.findByIdAndDelete(req.params.id);
  res.status(204).end();
});</pre>`},{q:'Compare Convex query, mutation, action and internalMutation functions, and explain six "Do vs Don\'t" rules for writing secure, high-performance Convex backends.',pts:['query: reactive read, no DB write, no external API','mutation: transactional DB write, no external API','action: external APIs (AI, payments, email), writes via mutations','internalMutation: server/CLI only (seeding, admin)','6 Do vs Don\'t rules: withIndex vs filter, v.id vs v.string, ctx.auth.getUserIdentity, clamp/validate untrusted client input, ConvexError, embed small data vs table sprawl'],model:`<p><b>Background:</b> Convex replaces traditional REST boilerplate with typed TypeScript server functions and reactive real-time queries, but engineers must still choose the right function primitive and enforce server-side security and indexing.</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b> Convex provides three main function types plus internal variants: (1) <b><code>query</code></b> reads data reactively and caches results, but cannot write to the DB or call external APIs; (2) <b><code>mutation</code></b> writes data (<code>insert</code>, <code>patch</code>, <code>delete</code>) inside an atomic transaction, preventing race conditions, and cannot call external APIs; (3) <b><code>action</code></b> calls third-party services (OpenAI, Paystack, Resend) and persists results by calling mutations; (4) <b><code>internalMutation</code></b> is callable only from other server functions or the CLI (<code>npx convex run</code>), keeping seeding and admin tasks off the public client API. Six core rules govern production Convex code: use <b><code>.withIndex()</code></b> instead of full-table <b><code>.filter()</code></b> scans; use <b><code>v.id('table')</code></b> instead of <code>v.string()</code> for foreign keys; embed small, always-read-together arrays (like book chapters) instead of over-splitting tables; never trust the client—always check <b><code>ctx.auth.getUserIdentity()</code></b> and clamp/validate inputs inside mutations; use <b><code>.unique()</code></b> on an index for one-row-per-user lookups; and throw <b><code>ConvexError</code></b> for clean client error handling.</p><p><b>Scenario:</b> when a user saves a bookmark in the audiobook app, the public <code>bookmarks:add</code> mutation verifies <code>getUserIdentity()</code>, checks that the <code>v.id('books')</code> document exists, queries existing chapter bookmarks via the <code>by_user_book_chapter_time</code> index, and throws a <code>ConvexError</code> if the 10-bookmark cap is reached.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> typed schemas and indexes guarantee fast, sorted reads in queries, while transactional mutations and authentication guards stop malicious or concurrent clients from corrupting state.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> treat every client call as untrusted, index every lookup field, and separate reactive reads (<code>query</code>), atomic writes (<code>mutation</code>), admin tasks (<code>internalMutation</code>) and external network calls (<code>action</code>).</p>`}]},
{id:'t5',title:'Mobile development',why:'React Native + Expo terms from the tutorial sheet.',
cards:[
{h:'Approaches',b:`<table><tr><th>Native</th><th>Cross-platform</th><th>Web/PWA</th></tr><tr><td>Kotlin/Java (Android), Swift (iOS): best performance</td><td>React Native, Flutter: one codebase</td><td>Runs in browser, installable</td></tr></table>`},
{h:'React Native + Expo terms',b:`<p><b>React Native</b>: build native apps with React components (View, Text, Button). <b>Expo</b>: toolchain around RN. <b>Expo Go</b>: app to preview your project on a phone via QR code. <b>Component</b>, <b>state</b>, <b>route</b> (Expo Router screens). <b>EAS</b> (Expo Application Services) builds and submits. <b>APK</b> = installable Android package; <b>AAB</b> = Android App Bundle for Play Store. <b>Prebuild</b> generates native android/ios folders. <b>Gradle</b> = Android build system; <b>Android SDK</b>; <b>dev build</b> = custom Expo Go with your native modules; <b>ADB</b> = Android Debug Bridge.</p><p>Store fees: Google Play <b>$25 one-time</b>; Apple Developer <b>$99/year</b>.</p>`},
{h:'Why Expo? Setup, project commands & SDK mismatch',b:`<p><b>Why React Native + Expo?</b> Write one JavaScript/TypeScript codebase for Android and iOS, test on a real phone via <b>Expo Go</b> without installing Android Studio, and build for iPhone in the cloud without owning a Mac.</p><p><b>Four prerequisites:</b> Node.js LTS (<code>node -v</code>), VS Code (+ Expo Tools extension), Expo Go on your phone, and a free Expo account at <code>expo.dev</code>.</p><pre>npx create-expo-app@latest MyFirstApp
cd MyFirstApp
npx expo start          # shows QR code (Android: Expo Go; iPhone: Camera app)
npx expo start --tunnel # use when phone and PC are not on the same Wi-Fi
npx expo start -c       # clear Metro bundler cache (press w for web, r to reload)</pre><div class="trap"><b>SDK version mismatch:</b> if the newest Expo SDK is ahead of the Expo Go app on the store, either create the project with the SDK version Expo Go supports, or build a <b>development build</b> (Step 8B) which removes the mismatch completely.</div>`},
{h:'Folder structure, Expo Router & core React Native components',b:`<pre>MyFirstApp/
├── app/               &lt;- every file here is a SCREEN (file-based routing)
│   ├── _layout.tsx    &lt;- controls navigation (stack, tabs)
│   ├── index.tsx      &lt;- home screen (/)
│   └── details.tsx    &lt;- /details screen
├── assets/            &lt;- images and fonts
├── components/        &lt;- reusable UI pieces
├── app.json           &lt;- app name, slug, icon, package / bundleIdentifier
└── package.json       &lt;- installed packages</pre><p><b>Clean starter code:</b> <code>npm run reset-project</code>. Saving a file updates the phone instantly via <b>Fast Refresh</b>.</p><table><tr><th>Piece</th><th>Purpose</th></tr><tr><td><code>View</code></td><td>Container, like a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> on the web</td></tr><tr><td><code>Text</code></td><td>All text <b>must</b> go inside a <code>&lt;Text&gt;</code> component</td></tr><tr><td><code>Pressable</code></td><td>Tappable area (button) using <code>onPress={() =&gt; ...}</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>StyleSheet.create</code></td><td>Styling in JS (Flexbox defaults to column; <code>paddingVertical</code>, <code>paddingHorizontal</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>Link</code> &amp; <code>useRouter</code></td><td>From <code>expo-router</code>: <code>&lt;Link href="/details"&gt;</code> and <code>const router = useRouter(); router.back();</code></td></tr></table>`},
{h:'app.json identifiers & Cloud (EAS) vs Local builds',b:`<p>In <code>app.json</code>, <code>android.package</code> and <code>ios.bundleIdentifier</code> (e.g. <code>com.yourname.myfirstapp</code>, lowercase, no spaces) <b>cannot be changed after you publish</b>.</p><pre>npm install -g eas-cli
eas login
eas build:configure
eas build --platform android --profile preview    # Cloud APK download link
eas build --platform android --profile production # Store AAB build
eas submit</pre><table><tr><th>Criteria</th><th>Cloud (EAS, Step 8A)</th><th>Local (Step 8B)</th></tr><tr><td>Setup</td><td>Almost none</td><td>Heavy (Android Studio + SDK + JDK 17, several GB)</td></tr><tr><td>Speed</td><td>Can wait in a queue on the free plan</td><td>No queue, but first Gradle build takes 10–30 min</td></tr><tr><td>Internet</td><td>Needed throughout</td><td>Needed for setup &amp; first Gradle download, less after</td></tr><tr><td>Computer needed</td><td>Any PC</td><td>8 GB RAM minimum, 16 GB recommended</td></tr><tr><td>iPhone builds</td><td>Works from Windows or Linux</td><td><b>Mac with Xcode only</b></td></tr><tr><td>Control</td><td>Limited</td><td>Full access to native code</td></tr></table>`},
{h:'Local Android setup, USB debugging, dev builds & Gradle release builds',b:`<p><b>Local setup (Step 8B):</b> install Android Studio (Android SDK, SDK Build-Tools, SDK Platform-Tools, SDK Command-line Tools) + <b>JDK 17</b> (<code>java -version</code>). Set <code>ANDROID_HOME</code> to <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Android\\Sdk</code> (Windows) or <code>$HOME/Android/Sdk</code> (Linux) / <code>$HOME/Library/Android/sdk</code> (Mac) and add <code>platform-tools</code> to <code>PATH</code> (check with <code>adb --version</code>).</p><p><b>Connect real phone:</b> Settings → About phone → tap <b>Build number 7 times</b> to unlock Developer options → enable <b>USB debugging</b> → plug in data cable → accept prompt → run <code>adb devices</code> (must say <code>device</code>, not <code>unauthorized</code>).</p><pre>npx expo install expo-dev-client
npx expo run:android    # runs prebuild, compiles with Gradle, installs dev build
npx expo prebuild       # generates native android/ and ios/ folders
cd android &amp;&amp; ./gradlew assembleRelease  # outputs app-release.apk
./gradlew bundleRelease                  # outputs app-release.aab for Google Play
eas build --platform android --profile preview --local  # macOS/Linux (or WSL)</pre><div class="trap"><b>Signing &amp; CNG warning:</b> by default, local release builds are signed with a <b>debug key</b> (rejected by Play Store; you must create and back up a <b>release keystore</b>—if lost, you can never update your store listing). Under <b>Continuous Native Generation (CNG)</b>, <code>android/</code> and <code>ios/</code> are in <code>.gitignore</code> and wiped by <code>npx expo prebuild --clean</code>; put native settings in <code>app.json</code> or config plugins. Free Apple ID sideloaded apps expire after <b>7 days</b>; new personal Google Play accounts ($25 one-time) must run a <b>2-week closed test</b> with real testers before production release.</div>`},
{h:'Mobile troubleshooting cheat sheet',b:`<table><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Fix from the tutorial</th></tr><tr><td>App won't load on phone</td><td>Check same Wi-Fi, or run <code>npx expo start --tunnel</code></td></tr><tr><td>"Project is incompatible with this version of Expo Go"</td><td>SDK mismatch: match Expo Go's SDK version or build a development build</td></tr><tr><td>Weird errors that make no sense</td><td>Clear bundler cache: <code>npx expo start -c</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>SDK location not found</code></td><td><code>ANDROID_HOME</code> isn't set; set it and restart terminal</td></tr><tr><td><code>adb</code> is not recognized</td><td>Add <code>platform-tools</code> to <code>PATH</code> and reopen terminal</td></tr><tr><td><code>adb devices</code> shows nothing / <code>unauthorized</code></td><td>Use a data cable (not charge-only), enable USB debugging, or unlock phone and accept prompt</td></tr><tr><td>Gradle runs out of memory</td><td>Add <code>org.gradle.jvmargs=-Xmx4g</code> to <code>android/gradle.properties</code></td></tr><tr><td>App name or icon didn't update</td><td>Run <code>npx expo prebuild --clean</code> and rebuild</td></tr></table>`}],
recall:[['What is Expo Go?','An app that runs your Expo project on a phone for testing, via QR code.'],['APK vs AAB?','APK is an installable Android package; AAB is the bundle format uploaded to Google Play.'],['What is EAS?','Expo Application Services: cloud build and submission.'],['Store developer fees?','Google Play $25 once; Apple $99 per year.'],['What does ADB do?','Android Debug Bridge: communicates with a device for installing and debugging.'],['How do you start an Expo dev server when your phone and laptop cannot see each other on Wi-Fi?','Run npx expo start --tunnel.'],['What is the one rule of Expo Router inside the app/ folder?','Every file inside app/ becomes a screen (app/index.tsx is /, app/details.tsx is /details, and app/_layout.tsx controls navigation).'],['Which command removes the starter demo code in a fresh Expo project?','npm run reset-project.'],['What are the React Native equivalents of <div>, text nodes, <button> and CSS stylesheets?','View (container), Text (all text must be wrapped in <Text>), Pressable (with onPress), and StyleSheet.create.'],['Which two fields in app.json can never be changed after publishing to the app stores?','android.package and ios.bundleIdentifier.'],['How do you unlock Developer Options on an Android phone for USB debugging?','Go to Settings -> About phone and tap Build number 7 times, then enable USB debugging in Developer options.'],['What four steps does npx expo run:android perform?','1. Runs prebuild to generate the native android/ folder, 2. Compiles the app with Gradle, 3. Installs it on the connected phone/emulator, 4. Starts the dev server.'],['Gradle command for a shareable release APK vs a Google Play AAB?','./gradlew assembleRelease builds app-release.apk; ./gradlew bundleRelease builds app-release.aab.'],['Why does Google Play reject a default local Gradle release build, and what happens if you lose your release keystore?','It is signed with a debug key by default. You must sign with a release keystore, and if you lose that keystore you can never update the app under the same Play Store listing.'],['What is Continuous Native Generation (CNG) in Expo, and why shouldn\'t you hand-edit files inside android/ or ios/?','Expo generates android/ and ios/ from app.json and config plugins; running npx expo prebuild --clean wipes those folders and erases any manual edits.'],['What is the testing requirement for new personal Google Play accounts, and how long do free Apple ID installs last on an iPhone?','New personal Google Play accounts ($25 one-time) must run a closed test with real testers for about 2 weeks; free Apple ID installs via Xcode expire after 7 days (vs $99/year paid account).'],['How do you fix Gradle running out of memory or app icon changes not showing up in a local build?','Add org.gradle.jvmargs=-Xmx4g to android/gradle.properties for memory; run npx expo prebuild --clean and rebuild for app.json name/icon changes.']],
quiz:[{k:'mcq',q:'Google Play requires which upload format for new apps?',o:['APK','AAB','IPA','EXE'],a:1},{k:'gap',q:'The Android build system is ___.',a:'gradle'},{k:'tf',q:'Apple\'s developer programme costs $25 once.',a:false},{k:'mcq',q:'Which Expo command starts the dev server over a tunnel when Wi-Fi blocks local connections?',o:['npx expo start --tunnel','npx expo prebuild --clean','eas build --local','npm run reset-project'],a:0},{k:'mcq',q:'In an Expo Router project, creating app/details.tsx automatically creates which route?',o:['/app/details','/details','/index/details','It does nothing until registered in server.js'],a:1},{k:'tf',q:'In React Native, you can place raw text strings directly inside a <View> without a <Text> wrapper.',a:false,w:'All text in React Native must go inside a <Text> component.'},{k:'mcq',q:'How many times do you tap "Build number" in Android Settings to unlock Developer options?',o:['3 times','5 times','7 times','10 times'],a:2},{k:'mcq',q:'Which Gradle command compiles an Android App Bundle (.aab) required for Google Play uploads?',o:['./gradlew assembleDebug','./gradlew assembleRelease','./gradlew bundleRelease','npx expo start -c'],a:2},{k:'mcq',q:'What happens if you hand-edit files in android/ and later run npx expo prebuild --clean?',o:['Expo merges your edits into app.json','Your hand-edits are wiped because android/ is regenerated from app.json (Continuous Native Generation)','Gradle refuses to run','The app converts to Flutter'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'How can a developer on Windows build an iOS app with React Native + Expo?',o:['Run npx expo run:ios locally on Windows','Use EAS Cloud build (eas build --platform ios), since local iOS builds require a Mac with Xcode','Install Xcode on Windows','Use ./gradlew bundleRelease'],a:1},{k:'gap',q:'To verify that your computer sees your USB-connected Android phone, run ___ devices.',a:'adb'},{k:'mcq',q:'New personal Google Play developer accounts must complete what step before publishing to production?',o:['Pay $99 every month','Run a closed test with real testers for about 2 weeks','Own a Mac computer','Write the app in Swift'],a:1}],
theory:[{q:'Compare native and cross-platform mobile development and describe the Expo workflow from code to Play Store.',pts:['Native pros/cons','Cross-platform pros/cons','Create project','Test in Expo Go','EAS build AAB','Submit; $25 fee'],model:`<p>Native gives best performance and API access but two codebases; cross-platform shares one codebase at some performance cost. Expo: create the app, test via Expo Go, use a dev build for native modules, build an AAB with EAS, submit to Google Play ($25 one-time).</p>`},{q:'Compare EAS Cloud builds (Step 8A) with Local builds (Step 8B) across the six tutorial criteria, and explain Continuous Native Generation (CNG) and release keystore signing.',pts:['6 criteria: setup, speed, internet, computer specs (8/16GB RAM), iPhone builds (Windows/Linux vs Mac+Xcode), control','EAS commands (eas login, eas build:configure, eas build --platform android --profile preview/production)','Local build steps (Android Studio, JDK 17, ANDROID_HOME, adb devices, npx expo run:android, ./gradlew assembleRelease vs bundleRelease)','Continuous Native Generation (CNG): android/ and ios/ generated from app.json, wiped by prebuild --clean','Release signing warning: debug key vs release keystore backup + store requirements ($25 + 2-week closed test vs $99/yr)'],model:`<p><b>Background:</b> Expo Go is only for development previews. To ship a real installable mobile app, an Expo developer must compile native binaries either in Expo's cloud (EAS, Step 8A) or locally on their own machine (Step 8B).</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b> the tutorial compares Cloud (EAS) and Local builds across six criteria: (1) <b>Setup:</b> EAS needs almost none (<code>npm install -g eas-cli</code>, <code>eas login</code>, <code>eas build:configure</code>), whereas local requires several GB of Android Studio, Android SDK tools, <b>JDK 17</b> and <code>ANDROID_HOME</code> / <code>platform-tools</code> environment variables; (2) <b>Speed:</b> free-tier EAS builds can wait in a cloud queue, whereas local builds have no queue (though the first Gradle download takes 10–30 minutes); (3) <b>Internet:</b> EAS needs internet throughout, while local needs it mainly for initial setup; (4) <b>Computer needed:</b> EAS runs from any laptop, while local requires at least <b>8 GB RAM (16 GB recommended)</b>; (5) <b>iPhone builds:</b> EAS can build iOS from Windows or Linux, whereas local iOS builds require a <b>Mac with Xcode</b>; (6) <b>Control:</b> local gives full direct access to native tooling (<code>./gradlew assembleRelease</code> for APK, <code>./gradlew bundleRelease</code> for Play Store AAB). Under <b>Continuous Native Generation (CNG)</b>, Expo generates <code>android/</code> and <code>ios/</code> from <code>app.json</code>; running <code>npx expo prebuild --clean</code> wipes hand-edits, so native config belongs in <code>app.json</code>. Finally, local builds default to a <b>debug key</b>; Play Store uploads require a backed-up <b>release keystore</b> (losing it permanently blocks future updates to that listing).</p><p><b>Scenario:</b> a student on a 4 GB Windows laptop uses <code>eas build --platform android --profile preview</code> to generate a test APK in the cloud and later switches to <code>--profile production</code> to produce a signed AAB for Google Play's 2-week closed test.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> Expo Router and Fast Refresh speed up daily coding, CNG keeps native folders reproducible from <code>app.json</code>, and EAS or local Gradle turns the project into signed APK/AAB artifacts.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> pick EAS Cloud when hardware is weak, internet is steady or building iOS from Windows; pick local Gradle builds when you have a 16 GB machine and want zero queue times.</p>`}]},
{id:'t6',title:'Deployment',why:'Hosting models, CI/CD, containers, environments.',
cards:[
{h:'Deployment concepts',b:`<p>Environments: development → staging → production. Hosting: static (Netlify, Vercel, GitHub Pages), PaaS (Render, Heroku), IaaS (VMs on AWS/Azure), containers (<b>Docker</b> images, Kubernetes orchestration), serverless functions.</p><p><b>CI/CD</b>: continuous integration (build and test on every push) and continuous delivery/deployment (automatic release). Environment variables hold secrets; never commit .env. Domain + DNS + HTTPS (TLS certificates).</p>`},
{h:'Why deployment matters & Web vs Mobile deployment',b:`<p><b>Deployment</b> is the bridge between <i>"it works on my machine"</i> and <i>"real users on real devices"</i>. Building an app is only the first half; it only delivers value when users can access it, and mistakes cost time, money and user trust.</p><p><b>Web deployment steps:</b> prepare code → configure for production → transfer to production environment → launch publicly.</p><p><b>Mobile deployment extras:</b> developer accounts and metadata, store reviews (Android simple review vs iOS stricter, longer review), beta testing (internal/external users), <b>device fragmentation</b> (many Android versions/screens), app size, permissions, and iterative vs big release update strategies.</p><table><tr><th>Web apps</th><th>Mobile apps</th></tr><tr><td>1. Easier to deploy/iterate (just update server)</td><td>1. More constraints (app stores, device versions, offline installs)</td></tr><tr><td>2. Immediate URL, instant updates for all users</td><td>2. Version distribution, store review, users must install/update</td></tr><tr><td>3. Low cost of distribution</td><td>3. High cost of distribution</td></tr></table>`},
{h:'Deployment best practices, common pitfalls & post-deployment',b:`<table><tr><th>Best Practices (Slides)</th><th>Common Pitfalls (Slides)</th></tr><tr><td>Use version control (Git)</td><td>"Works on my machine" but fails in prod due to config mismatch</td></tr><tr><td>Environment variables &amp; separate dev/staging/prod configs</td><td>Manual deployments causing config drift and human error</td></tr><tr><td>Automate builds &amp; deployments (CI/CD); minimise manual steps</td><td>Neglecting rollback or hotfix planning</td></tr><tr><td>Monitor performance and errors in production</td><td>Ignoring monitoring so you don't know when things break</td></tr><tr><td>Rollback strategy: know how to revert quickly</td><td>Deploying straight to production without testing/staging</td></tr><tr><td>Security: HTTPS, CORS, access controls</td><td>Mobile pitfalls: releasing a bad version, missing store rules, device fragmentation bugs</td></tr><tr><td>Mobile: handle store updates, versioning, clear changelog</td><td></td></tr></table><p><b>Monitoring, maintenance &amp; iteration:</b> deployment isn't the end—use logging, analytics and alerts; plan feature updates, bug fixes and security patches; handle deprecated mobile APIs; and document the deployment process for team continuity.</p>`},
{h:'Real-world example: Osisi production stack & future trends',b:`<p><b>Osisi's production stack (from the Deployment slides):</b></p><ul><li><b>Frontend:</b> Next.js (React) deployed via <b>Vercel</b></li><li><b>Backend:</b> <b>Convex</b></li><li><b>Static assets:</b> <b>Cloudflare</b> for images</li><li><b>Transactional emails:</b> <b>Resend</b></li><li><b>Payments:</b> <b>Paystack</b></li><li><b>Authentication:</b> <b>Auth0</b></li></ul><p><b>Emerging topics &amp; future trends:</b> (1) Containerization and orchestration (Kubernetes) for scaling · (2) Serverless / edge deployments (faster, cheaper) · (3) Hybrid web-mobile apps (<b>PWA</b>) narrowing the gap · (4) Continuous deployment and automatic rollbacks · (5) Security and compliance.</p>`}],
recall:[['CI vs CD?','CI builds and tests each change; CD automatically delivers/deploys passing builds.'],['Docker image vs container?','An image is the packaged template; a container is a running instance of it.'],['Where should API keys live in deployment?','In environment variables / secret stores, not in the repository.'],['Three differences between Web app deployment and Mobile app deployment on the slides.','Web: update server once, immediate URL/instant updates, low distribution cost. Mobile: app store review and device/offline constraints, users must install version updates, high distribution cost.'],['Name four mobile-specific deployment challenges from the slides.','Stricter iOS store review vs Android, device fragmentation across Android versions/devices, app size and permissions, and managing version updates/changelogs.'],['Six common deployment pitfalls listed on the slides.','"Works on my machine" config mismatch, manual deployments causing drift/human error, neglecting rollback/hotfix planning, ignoring monitoring, deploying without testing/staging, and mobile store/fragmentation mistakes.'],['Name every service in the Osisi real-world production stack (6 components).','Frontend: Next.js (React) on Vercel; Backend: Convex; Static assets/images: Cloudflare; Transactional emails: Resend; Payments: Paystack; Authentication: Auth0.'],['Five emerging deployment trends on the slides.','Containerization and orchestration (Kubernetes), serverless/edge deployments, hybrid web-mobile apps (PWAs), continuous deployment with automatic rollbacks, and heightened security/compliance.']],
quiz:[{k:'mcq',q:'Which hosts a static React build cheaply?',o:['Netlify/Vercel','A mainframe','FTP to a phone','Kubernetes only'],a:0},{k:'tf',q:'Staging should mirror production for final testing.',a:true},{k:'mcq',q:'In the Osisi production stack, which service handles transactional emails and which handles payments?',o:['SendGrid and Stripe','Resend and Paystack','Cloudflare and Auth0','Vercel and Convex'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'In the Osisi production stack, which service hosts static image assets and which handles authentication?',o:['Cloudflare (images) and Auth0 (authentication)','AWS S3 and Firebase','Vercel and Clerk','Netlify and Supabase'],a:0},{k:'mcq',q:'Why is mobile app deployment harder to iterate than web app deployment?',o:['Mobile apps cannot use Git','Mobile updates require app store review, version management, and users installing updates across fragmented devices','Web apps do not use servers','Mobile apps cannot use HTTPS'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'When an app runs locally but crashes in production because environment variables differ, this is which common pitfall?',o:['Device fragmentation','"Works on my machine" configuration mismatch','Over-monitoring','Progressive disclosure'],a:1},{k:'tf',q:'Once an application is deployed to production, the deployment lifecycle is finished.',a:false,w:'Deployment is ongoing: it requires monitoring, logging, alerts, security patches, updates and documentation.'},{k:'gap',q:'Hybrid web-mobile applications that narrow the gap between web and native deployment are called ___ (abbreviation).',a:'pwa',alt:['pwas','progressive web apps']}],
theory:[{q:'Describe a CI/CD pipeline for deploying a Node.js API.',pts:['Push to Git','CI: install, test, lint','Build Docker image','Deploy to staging','Approve → production','Secrets via env','Monitoring/rollback'],model:`<p>A push triggers GitHub Actions to install, lint and test; a Docker image is built and pushed; it deploys to staging, then production after approval. Secrets come from environment variables; monitoring and rollback complete the loop.</p>`},{q:'Contrast web and mobile deployment, list six deployment best practices and pitfalls, and describe the complete Osisi production stack from the lectures.',pts:['Web vs Mobile deployment (instant server update & low cost vs store review, versioning, device fragmentation, high cost)','6 best practices: Git, env vars + dev/staging/prod, CI/CD automation, monitoring/alerts, rollback strategy, security (HTTPS, CORS, access controls)','6 common pitfalls: works on my machine, manual drift, no rollback, no monitoring, skipping staging, mobile store/fragmentation bugs','Osisi stack: Next.js on Vercel, Convex backend, Cloudflare images, Resend emails, Paystack payments, Auth0 authentication'],model:`<p><b>Background:</b> deployment bridges the gap between local code and real users on real devices. A poor deployment pipeline causes downtime, security breaches and lost user trust.</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b> web and mobile deployment differ sharply: <b>web apps</b> update instantaneously on the server at an immediate URL with low distribution cost, whereas <b>mobile apps</b> face app-store gatekeepers (simple Android review vs stricter, slower iOS review), version fragmentation across devices, offline constraints and user-initiated updates. Six best practices prevent common pitfalls: (1) use <b>Git version control</b>; (2) separate dev, staging and production configs via <b>environment variables</b> (avoiding the <i>"works on my machine"</i> pitfall); (3) automate builds and deploys with <b>CI/CD</b> (avoiding manual drift and human error); (4) test in <b>staging</b> before production; (5) maintain a <b>rollback/hotfix strategy</b> and live <b>monitoring/alerts</b>; and (6) enforce <b>HTTPS, CORS and access controls</b>. In the lecturer's real-world case study, <b>Osisi</b> combines specialized managed platforms: <b>Next.js (React)</b> deployed on <b>Vercel</b> for the frontend, <b>Convex</b> for the reactive backend and database, <b>Cloudflare</b> for static image assets, <b>Resend</b> for transactional emails, <b>Paystack</b> for payments and <b>Auth0</b> for authentication.</p><p><b>Scenario:</b> when an Osisi engineer pushes a family-tree update to GitHub, Vercel builds a staging preview, Convex validates the schema, and monitoring alerts watch error rates so the team can roll back in one click if a regression appears.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> managed services (Vercel, Convex, Cloudflare, Auth0, Resend, Paystack) remove raw server maintenance, while CI/CD, staging environments and rollback plans keep releases safe.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> choose tools that fit the project scope without over-engineering, automate every release step, and treat deployment as an ongoing loop of monitoring and iteration.</p>`}]},
{id:'t7',title:'Deployment practical',why:'Commands you may be asked to write.',
cards:[
{h:'Commands (additional context)',b:`<div class="trap"><b>Additional context:</b> the lecturer's Deployment Practical deploys a <b>Vite/Next.js + Convex</b> app to <b>GitHub and Vercel</b>, not Docker. The cards below cover the exact GitHub, Convex and Vercel deployment steps from the slides.</div><pre># Dockerfile
FROM node:20-alpine
WORKDIR /app
COPY package*.json ./
RUN npm install
COPY . .
EXPOSE 3000
CMD ["node","server.js"]

docker build -t myapi .
docker run -p 3000:3000 myapi

npm run build          # front-end production build
eas build -p android   # Expo build</pre>`},
{h:'Deployment Practical: GitHub, Vite, Convex & Vercel workflow',b:`<p><b>Five prerequisites:</b> GitHub account, Vercel account, Git installed, Git connected to GitHub, Node.js installed.</p><ol><li><b>GitHub repo setup:</b> create repo <code>CSC-419-DEPLOYMENT</code> with Visibility = <b>Public</b>, Add README = <b>True</b>, Licence = <b>MIT Licence</b>. Copy clone link, create local folder <code>CSC_419/deployments</code>, clone and <code>cd CSC-419-DEPLOYMENT</code>.</li><li><b>Install Vite + React + TypeScript:</b><pre>npm create vite@latest my-app -- --template react-ts</pre>Select <b>ESLint</b> as linter, then <code>cd my-app</code>.</li><li><b>Install and initialise Convex:</b><pre>npm install convex
npx convex dev</pre>Log in / confirm in browser → create project <code>my-app</code> using <b>cloud deployment</b>. This creates the <code>convex/</code> folder and writes your deployment URL to <b><code>.env.local</code></b>.</li><li><b>Push to GitHub:</b><pre>git add .
git commit -m "added nextjs and convex project"
git push</pre></li><li><b>Deploy on Vercel:</b> Dashboard → <b>Add New → Project</b> → connect Git provider (GitHub/GitLab/BitBucket) → Import <code>CSC-419-DEPLOYMENT</code> → set <b>Root Directory</b> to <b><code>my-app</code></b> (and Framework Preset, e.g. Next.js/Vite) → click <b>Deploy</b> → Continue to Dashboard → <b>Visit</b>.</li></ol>`}],
recall:[['Which Dockerfile instruction sets the start command?','CMD.'],['What does -p 3000:3000 do?','Maps host port 3000 to container port 3000.'],['Name the five prerequisites for the Deployment Practical.','A GitHub account, a Vercel account, Git installed, Git connected to your GitHub account, and Node.js installed.'],['What exact repository name, visibility, README and licence settings are used in the Deployment Practical?','Name: CSC-419-DEPLOYMENT, Visibility: Public, Add README: True, Licence: MIT Licence.'],['What command scaffolds the React + TypeScript Vite app in the Deployment Practical, and which linter do you pick?','npm create vite@latest my-app -- --template react-ts, choosing ESLint.'],['Which two commands install Convex and start a Convex cloud dev deployment, and which two project artifacts do they create?','npm install convex and npx convex dev. They create the convex/ folder and the .env.local file.'],['When importing the monorepo into Vercel in the Deployment Practical, what must you set the Root Directory to?','my-app.']],
quiz:[{k:'mcq',q:'Which command creates an image from a Dockerfile?',o:['docker run','docker build','docker pull','docker ps'],a:1},{k:'gap',q:'The Dockerfile instruction that picks the base image is ___.',a:'from'},{k:'mcq',q:'In the Deployment Practical, which command scaffolds the React + TypeScript project named my-app?',o:['npx create-react-app my-app','npm create vite@latest my-app -- --template react-ts','docker init my-app','npx expo start my-app'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'Running npx convex dev in my-app generates which folder and environment file?',o:['server/ and config.json','convex/ and .env.local','dist/ and Dockerfile','android/ and app.json'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'When deploying CSC-419-DEPLOYMENT on Vercel, why must you edit the Root Directory setting to "my-app"?',o:['Because the Vite/Next.js app was created inside the my-app subfolder of the cloned repository','Because Vercel requires every repo to be named my-app','To disable HTTPS','To delete the convex folder'],a:0},{k:'gap',q:'In the Deployment Practical, the GitHub repository is created with the ___ Licence.',a:'mit',alt:['MIT']}],
theory:[{q:'Write a Dockerfile for a Node.js app and the commands to build and run it.',pts:['FROM','WORKDIR','COPY package + npm install','COPY source','EXPOSE','CMD','build/run commands'],model:`<p>See the card: FROM node:20-alpine, WORKDIR /app, copy package files, npm install, copy source, EXPOSE 3000, CMD node server.js; then docker build -t myapi . and docker run -p 3000:3000 myapi.</p>`},{q:'Write out the complete step-by-step commands and configuration from the Deployment Practical to take a new React + TypeScript + Convex project from a blank GitHub repository to a live Vercel URL.',pts:['5 prerequisites (GitHub, Vercel, Git, Git linked, Node.js)','GitHub repo CSC-419-DEPLOYMENT (Public, README, MIT) + git clone into CSC_419/deployments','npm create vite@latest my-app -- --template react-ts + ESLint','npm install convex + npx convex dev -> convex/ folder & .env.local','git add . / git commit / git push','Vercel Add New -> Project -> Import repo -> Root Directory: my-app -> Deploy -> Visit'],model:`<p><b>Background:</b> the Deployment Practical demonstrates a modern serverless web pipeline connecting GitHub source control, a Convex cloud backend and Vercel continuous frontend hosting.</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b> first ensure all five prerequisites are ready (GitHub account, Vercel account, Git installed and connected to GitHub, Node.js installed). Step 1: on GitHub, create a <b>Public</b> repository named <code>CSC-419-DEPLOYMENT</code> with a <b>README</b> and the <b>MIT Licence</b>, then clone it locally into <code>CSC_419/deployments</code> and <code>cd CSC-419-DEPLOYMENT</code>. Step 2: scaffold the frontend with <code>npm create vite@latest my-app -- --template react-ts</code> and select <b>ESLint</b>. Step 3: inside <code>my-app</code>, install the backend client with <code>npm install convex</code> and run <code>npx convex dev</code>, authenticating in the browser and selecting a cloud deployment for <code>my-app</code>; this generates the <code>convex/</code> directory and populates <code>.env.local</code> with the Convex URL. Step 4: stage, commit and push to GitHub using <code>git add .</code>, <code>git commit -m "added nextjs and convex project"</code> and <code>git push</code>. Step 5: in the Vercel dashboard, click <b>Add New → Project</b>, import <code>CSC-419-DEPLOYMENT</code> from GitHub, change the <b>Root Directory</b> setting to <code>my-app</code>, select the framework preset, and click <b>Deploy</b>.</p><p><b>Scenario:</b> once deployed, every subsequent <code>git push</code> to the repository automatically triggers Vercel to rebuild <code>my-app</code> and publish the updated UI against the live Convex deployment.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> Git and GitHub track source history, <code>npx convex dev</code> provisions the reactive backend and environment variables, and Vercel points at the <code>my-app</code> root directory to build and serve the frontend globally.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> setting the Vercel Root Directory to <code>my-app</code> and keeping Convex URLs in environment variables bridges local development to a live production URL in minutes.</p>`}]}
],
past:{title:'PAU-CSC 419 · 2025/2026 First Semester Examination (Feb 2026 · 2 Hours · Q1 compulsory + any other questions)',note:'Transcribed directly from Exam.pdf in the Blockchain folder. Note: prompt engineering, RAG and AI credit-scoring ethics appear on this paper even though no AI/Blockchain slide deck was in the folder; those sections in the model answers are flagged as additional context.',qs:[{h:'Q1a · MediConnect Convex Schema Audit',marks:8,q:'Case Study: MediConnect is a proposed telemedicine platform handling patient medical records (diagnoses, prescriptions, allergies, test results), doctor profiles/specialisations, appointment scheduling and video consultations, prescription management, insurance claim processing, real-time chat, and medical document storage (lab reports, X-rays).\n\nBelow is a proposed Convex schema:\n<pre>export default defineSchema({\n  users: defineTable({\n    email: v.string(), password: v.string(), fullName: v.string(),\n    role: v.string(), // "patient" or "doctor"\n    specialization: v.optional(v.string()), licenseNumber: v.optional(v.string()),\n    allergies: v.optional(v.string()), bloodType: v.optional(v.string()),\n    emergencyContact: v.optional(v.string()), insuranceProvider: v.optional(v.string()),\n    insurancePolicyNumber: v.optional(v.string()), doctorRating: v.optional(v.number()),\n    consultationFee: v.optional(v.number()),\n  }),\n  appointments: defineTable({\n    patientId: v.id("users"), doctorId: v.id("users"),\n    appointmentDate: v.string(), status: v.string(),\n    videoCallLink: v.optional(v.string()), notes: v.optional(v.string()),\n    prescriptionText: v.optional(v.string()), diagnosis: v.optional(v.string()),\n    testResults: v.optional(v.string()),\n  }),\n  messages: defineTable({\n    senderId: v.id("users"), receiverId: v.id("users"),\n    message: v.string(), timestamp: v.number(),\n  }),\n  medicalDocuments: defineTable({\n    userId: v.id("users"), documentUrl: v.string(), uploadDate: v.string(),\n  }),\n});</pre>Identify and explain at least 8 significant problems with this schema (normalisation, missing indexes, incorrect relationships, poor data validation, security). For each problem, explain why it is problematic and how you would fix it.',pts:['1. Plaintext password in users table (security) -> use Convex Auth / Auth0 / Clerk tokenIdentifier, never store raw password','2. Unnormalized single-table god object in users (patient + doctor fields mixed) -> split doctorProfiles and patientProfiles tables linked by userId: v.id("users")','3. Zero indexes defined on any table (.index missing) -> add by_email, by_patient, by_doctor_date, by_conversation, by_user indexes','4. Loose v.string() instead of strict unions/literals for role and status -> use v.union(v.literal("patient"), v.literal("doctor")) and status literals','5. Clinical data (prescriptionText, diagnosis, testResults) crammed into appointments as flat strings -> extract prescriptions, medicalRecords/diagnoses and insuranceClaims tables (or structured arrays)','6. allergies stored as a single flat v.optional(v.string()) -> use v.array(v.string()) or structured allergy objects','7. Dates stored as unvalidated v.string() (appointmentDate, uploadDate) -> use epoch milliseconds v.number() (or rely on Convex _creationTime) for reliable sorting and range queries','8. medicalDocuments uses raw documentUrl: v.string() with no doctor/appointment link or access control -> use Convex storage v.id("_storage"), link appointmentId/uploadedBy, and generate signed URLs via ctx.storage.getUrl()'],model:`<p><b>Background:</b> in Convex (and backend data modeling generally), a schema is the foundation of data integrity, query performance and security. A telemedicine platform handling protected health information must normalize distinct entities, index every query path and enforce strict validators.</p><p><b>Answer and solution (8 schema flaws and fixes):</b></p><ol><li><b>Storing plaintext/raw <code>password: v.string()</code> in <code>users</code> (Security):</b> if the table is leaked or returned by a query, user passwords are exposed. <i>Fix:</i> remove <code>password</code>; delegate authentication to Auth0, Clerk or Convex Auth and store <code>tokenIdentifier: v.string()</code> indexed with <code>.index("by_token", ["tokenIdentifier"])</code>.</li><li><b>Normalization violation in <code>users</code> (God table):</b> patient fields (<code>allergies, bloodType, insurancePolicyNumber</code>) and doctor fields (<code>specialization, licenseNumber, doctorRating, consultationFee</code>) are mixed as nullable optionals on every user. <i>Fix:</i> keep <code>users</code> for core identity and extract <code>patientProfiles</code> and <code>doctorProfiles</code> tables referencing <code>userId: v.id("users")</code>.</li><li><b>Complete absence of database indexes (Performance):</b> none of the four tables calls <code>.index(...)</code>, forcing full table scans (<code>.filter()</code>) on every lookup. <i>Fix:</i> add <code>.index("by_email", ["email"])</code> on <code>users</code>, <code>.index("by_patient", ["patientId"])</code> and <code>.index("by_doctor_date", ["doctorId", "appointmentDate"])</code> on <code>appointments</code>, <code>.index("by_sender_receiver", ["senderId", "receiverId", "timestamp"])</code> on <code>messages</code>, and <code>.index("by_user", ["userId"])</code> on <code>medicalDocuments</code>.</li><li><b>Unconstrained <code>v.string()</code> for finite enums (<code>role</code>, <code>status</code>):</b> any typo like <code>"doctr"</code> or <code>"cancellled"</code> will be accepted. <i>Fix:</i> use <code>v.union(v.literal("patient"), v.literal("doctor"), v.literal("admin"))</code> for <code>role</code> and <code>v.union(v.literal("scheduled"), v.literal("completed"), v.literal("cancelled"))</code> for appointment <code>status</code>.</li><li><b>Cramming clinical records into <code>appointments</code> (<code>prescriptionText, diagnosis, testResults</code>) &amp; missing Insurance Claims table:</b> a consultation can yield multiple prescriptions with dosages/refills, multiple lab results and an insurance claim workflow. <i>Fix:</i> create separate <code>prescriptions</code>, <code>labResults</code> and <code>insuranceClaims</code> tables linked via <code>appointmentId: v.id("appointments")</code> and <code>patientId: v.id("users")</code>.</li><li><b>Storing multi-valued <code>allergies</code> as a single string:</b> comma-separated strings cannot be validated or queried safely for drug-allergy checks. <i>Fix:</i> use <code>v.array(v.object({ substance: v.string(), severity: v.string() }))</code>.</li><li><b>Dates stored as <code>v.string()</code> (<code>appointmentDate</code>, <code>uploadDate</code>):</b> arbitrary date strings break chronological index sorting and timezone comparisons; also <code>timestamp</code> in <code>messages</code> duplicates Convex's automatic <code>_creationTime</code>. <i>Fix:</i> store timestamps as UTC epoch milliseconds <code>v.number()</code>.</li><li><b>Insecure <code>documentUrl: v.string()</code> in <code>medicalDocuments</code>:</b> storing public URLs exposes X-rays and lab reports without authorization checks and lacks metadata (document type, doctor who ordered it). <i>Fix:</i> store <code>storageId: v.id("_storage")</code>, <code>docType: v.union(v.literal("xray"), v.literal("lab"), v.literal("prescription"))</code>, <code>appointmentId: v.optional(v.id("appointments"))</code>, and issue short-lived URLs via <code>ctx.storage.getUrl(doc.storageId)</code> only after verifying authorization.</li></ol><p><b>Scenario:</b> when a patient opens MediConnect to view upcoming consultations, an indexed query on <code>by_patient</code> fetches only their appointments in milliseconds without scanning the whole hospital database or leaking doctor-only fields.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> splitting role profiles normalizes storage, <code>v.union(v.literal(...))</code> and <code>v.id(...)</code> enforce schema-level validation, indexes eliminate full table scans, and <code>_storage</code> IDs plus external auth protect medical privacy.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> a production Convex healthcare schema must never store passwords or public medical URLs, must normalize patient/doctor/clinical entities, and must index every foreign key and timestamp.</p>`},{h:'Q1b · Symptom-to-Specialisation AI Prompt Engineering Strategy',marks:7,q:'The platform wants to implement an AI assistant that helps patients describe their symptoms in natural language and suggests potential specialisations they should consult. Design a prompt engineering strategy for this feature, including:\n• The system message you would use\n• At least two example few-shot prompts\n• How you would handle medical disclaimers and liability concerns\n• Why you would or wouldn\'t use RAG for this feature',pts:['System message: role (triage routing assistant, NOT a doctor), strict prohibition on diagnosing or prescribing, output format (structured JSON with recommended specialization, urgency level, red-flag escalation, disclaimer)','Two few-shot examples: (1) non-urgent specialty routing (e.g. persistent skin rash -> Dermatology) and (2) emergency red-flag symptoms (e.g. crushing chest pain + left arm numbness -> Emergency Care / Cardiology immediate escalation)','Medical disclaimers & liability: pre-chat consent banner, hard-coded UI disclaimer outside LLM output, emergency red-flag bypass, audit logging, human-in-the-loop doctor review','Why or why not RAG: use lightweight RAG over MediConnect\'s verified list of available hospital specialisations, doctor profiles and approved clinical triage protocols so it never hallucinates unavailable specialties (while avoiding unverified open-web RAG)'],model:`<div class="trap"><b>Additional context:</b> prompt engineering and RAG are not covered in the 7 slide decks in the Blockchain folder, but they are examined directly here in the Feb 2026 past paper.</div><p><b>Background:</b> in a telemedicine app, a symptom router must map free-text patient descriptions to the right medical speciality while strictly avoiding unauthorized medical diagnosis or dangerous delay during emergencies.</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b></p><ul><li><b>1. System message:</b><pre>You are MediConnect's intake triage routing assistant. Your ONLY job is to help patients identify which medical specialisation on MediConnect to book (e.g., Cardiology, Dermatology, Pediatrics, Orthopedics, General Practice) and assess urgency (Routine, Soon, Emergency).
RULES:
1. NEVER diagnose conditions, prescribe medication, or give treatment instructions.
2. If symptoms match emergency red flags (chest pain, difficulty breathing, stroke signs, severe bleeding, suicidal ideation), set urgency="EMERGENCY" and instruct the patient to go to the nearest emergency room immediately.
3. Ask at most 2 clarifying questions (duration, severity) if the input is too vague.
4. Respond in strict JSON: {"suggestedSpecializations": [...], "urgency": "Routine|Soon|Emergency", "reasoning": "...", "questionsToAskDoctor": [...], "disclaimer": "..."}.</pre></li><li><b>2. Two few-shot examples:</b><pre>Example 1 (Routine routing):
User: "I've had an itchy, scaly red patch on my elbow for three weeks that isn't going away with lotion."
Assistant: {"suggestedSpecializations": ["Dermatology", "General Practice"], "urgency": "Routine", "reasoning": "Persistent skin rashes and plaques are evaluated by a dermatologist or GP.", "questionsToAskDoctor": ["Could this be eczema, psoriasis, or a fungal infection?", "What topical treatments are safe?"], "disclaimer": "This is a booking suggestion, not a medical diagnosis."}

Example 2 (Emergency red-flag escalation):
User: "My father is 58, sweating heavily, and says his chest feels squeezed with pain spreading to his jaw."
Assistant: {"suggestedSpecializations": ["Emergency Medicine", "Cardiology"], "urgency": "EMERGENCY", "reasoning": "Chest pressure radiating to the jaw with sweating is a critical cardiac red flag requiring immediate emergency intervention rather than an online video booking.", "questionsToAskDoctor": [], "disclaimer": "EMERGENCY: Call emergency services or go to the nearest hospital ER immediately."}</pre></li><li><b>3. Medical disclaimers &amp; liability safeguards:</b> render a mandatory click-to-accept informed-consent modal before chat opens; inject the legal disclaimer deterministically in the React UI rather than trusting the LLM not to drop it; run a deterministic keyword/classifier guardrail for emergency red flags; log all prompts and responses for clinical audit; and frame outputs as <i>"specialisations to consult"</i> rather than diagnoses.</li><li><b>4. Why use (bounded) RAG:</b> a pure LLM might recommend a specialty MediConnect has no doctors for (e.g. "Neuro-ophthalmology"). Using <b>bounded RAG</b> against MediConnect's live Convex <code>doctorProfiles</code> catalogue and a curated clinical symptom-to-department taxonomy grounds suggestions in actually bookable doctors and hospital guidelines, without needed full open-ended medical-textbook RAG.</li></ul><p><b>Scenario:</b> a patient in Lagos types <i>"sharp tooth pain when drinking cold water"</i>; theConvex action retrieves MediConnect's active Dental and Maxillofacial clinics via RAG, runs the few-shot system prompt, and displays a "Dentistry (Soon)" booking card with a pinned legal disclaimer.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> the system prompt and few-shot examples constrain tone and JSON structure, deterministic UI disclaimers handle legal liability, and bounded RAG links the output directly to real MediConnect doctors.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> safe clinical AI combines strict negative constraints (never diagnose), red-flag emergency escalation, deterministic UI disclaimers and catalogue-grounded retrieval.</p>`},{h:'Q1c · MediConnect Startup Deployment Strategy',marks:5,q:'Propose an appropriate deployment strategy for MediConnect, considering:\n• Expected traffic patterns (peak during business hours)\n• The requirement for 99.9% uptime\n• Budget constraints of a startup\nInclude your choice of hosting platform, deployment pipeline, and monitoring strategy.',pts:['Hosting platform: Osisi-style serverless stack — Next.js on Vercel + Convex backend + Cloudflare CDN/storage (scales automatically for daytime peaks, near-zero idle cost for startup budget, 99.9%+ managed SLA)','Deployment pipeline: Git/GitHub -> automated CI/CD (lint, typecheck, unit/integration tests) -> staging/preview environment -> production deploy with zero-downtime atomic switch and instant rollback','Environment & security: strict dev/staging/prod separation, secrets in env vars, HTTPS/TLS, CORS, auth via Auth0/Clerk','Monitoring strategy: error tracking (Sentry), uptime health pings & latency alerts, Convex function logs/dashboard, audit logs for medical access'],model:`<p><b>Background:</b> MediConnect is an early-stage healthcare startup that experiences spiky daytime consultation traffic, needs 99.9% high availability (under 44 minutes of downtime per month), and cannot afford idle dedicated server clusters or a full-time DevOps team.</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b></p><ul><li><b>1. Hosting platform (Managed Serverless — the Osisi Stack):</b> deploy the React/Next.js frontend on <b>Vercel</b> and the backend/database on <b>Convex</b>, with <b>Cloudflare</b> for static assets/CDN, <b>Auth0/Clerk</b> for authentication and <b>Resend</b> for notifications. Serverless auto-scales instantaneously during 8am–6pm clinic peaks and scales down to near-zero cost overnight, meeting both the startup budget and 99.9% multi-AZ uptime without Kubernetes overhead.</li><li><b>2. Deployment pipeline (CI/CD):</b> developers work on feature branches in <b>GitHub</b>. Every pull request triggers GitHub Actions to run TypeScript checks, ESLint and unit/integration tests, while Vercel and Convex automatically spin up an isolated <b>staging/preview environment</b>. Merging to <code>main</code> runs <code>npx convex deploy</code> and promotes the Vercel build atomically with zero downtime and one-click <b>rollback</b> if a release fails. Secrets live strictly in separate dev, staging and production environment variables.</li><li><b>3. Monitoring &amp; alerting strategy:</b> integrate <b>Sentry</b> and Vercel/Convex observability logs to capture runtime exceptions and slow queries; configure automated uptime pings with Slack/SMS alerts whenever error rates exceed 1% or p95 latency spikes; and keep immutable audit logs of all medical record reads.</li></ul><p><b>Scenario:</b> at 9:30 AM on Monday when hundreds of patients join video consultations simultaneously, Vercel edge functions and Convex scale horizontally without manual intervention, while an afternoon bug in a new UI push is rolled back to the previous immutable Vercel deployment in under 10 seconds.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> pay-as-you-go serverless hosting handles business-hour peaks cheaply, CI/CD + staging prevents broken pushes from reaching production, and real-time alerting + instant rollback protect the 99.9% SLA.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> following the lecture rule to <i>choose tools that match project scope and avoid over-engineering</i>, a Vercel + Convex serverless pipeline gives MediConnect enterprise uptime on a startup budget.</p>`},{h:'Q2a · SmartInventory Convex Mutation Code Review',marks:10,q:'Case Study: SmartInventory is building a warehouse management system for a logistics company operating 15 warehouses across Nigeria. A junior engineer implemented this Convex mutation:\n<pre>export const updateInventory = mutation({\n  args: {\n    productId: v.string(),\n    warehouseId: v.string(),\n    quantityChange: v.number(), // positive or negative\n  },\n  handler: async (ctx, args) =&gt; {\n    const inventory = await ctx.db\n      .query("inventory")\n      .filter(q =&gt; q.and(\n        q.eq(q.field("productId"), args.productId),\n        q.eq(q.field("warehouseId"), args.warehouseId)\n      ))\n      .first();\n    if (inventory) {\n      await ctx.db.patch(inventory._id, {\n        quantity: inventory.quantity + args.quantityChange,\n        updatedAt: Date.now(),\n      });\n    } else {\n      await ctx.db.insert("inventory", {\n        productId: args.productId, warehouseId: args.warehouseId,\n        quantity: args.quantityChange, updatedAt: Date.now(),\n      });\n    }\n    return { success: true };\n  },\n});</pre>Identify and explain at least 8 significant problems or weaknesses focusing on (1) Authentication and Authorisation, (2) Data Validation and Business Rules, and (3) Performance and Optimisation. For each: describe the problem, explain why it is dangerous/inefficient in a 15-warehouse system, and propose a concrete fix.',pts:['1. No Authentication check (ctx.auth.getUserIdentity() missing) -> anyone on the internet can alter warehouse stock','2. No Authorisation / warehouse-scoped role check -> a viewer or staff at Lagos warehouse can edit Kano warehouse stock','3. Using v.string() instead of v.id("products") and v.id("warehouses") -> no foreign-key type safety','4. No Existence validation (ctx.db.get(args.productId) / warehouseId) -> creates orphan inventory rows for deleted/fake products','5. Negative stock bug on patch & insert (inventory.quantity + quantityChange < 0, or inserting negative quantityChange for non-existent stock) -> dispatching more stock than exists creates impossible negative inventory','6. Invalid quantityChange values (0, fractional floats like 1.37, non-finite values, huge numbers) -> validate Number.isInteger(args.quantityChange) && args.quantityChange !== 0','7. Full-table scan with .filter(...) instead of .withIndex("by_warehouse_product", ...) -> O(N) scan across all 15 warehouses on every dispatch','8. Using .first() without a uniqueness constraint instead of indexed .unique(), plus no audit log / stockMovement transaction record -> silent duplicate rows and zero accountability for missing stock'],model:`<p><b>Background:</b> in Convex, the client is always untrusted. A warehouse stock mutation directly controls physical inventory across 15 Nigerian depots; omitting authentication, business-rule validation or database indexes invites theft, negative stock corruption and severe performance bottlenecks.</p><p><b>Answer and solution (8 problems grouped across the 3 required areas):</b></p><ol><li><b>Missing Authentication (Auth &amp; Authz):</b> the handler never calls <code>const identity = await ctx.auth.getUserIdentity()</code>. <i>Why dangerous:</i> any unauthenticated script can invoke <code>updateInventory</code> and wipe out stock figures. <i>Fix:</i> check <code>if (!identity) throw new ConvexError("Not signed in");</code> at line 1.</li><li><b>Missing Role &amp; Warehouse-level Authorisation (Auth &amp; Authz):</b> even a logged-in user is not checked for role (viewer vs warehouse manager) or whether they are assigned to <code>args.warehouseId</code>. <i>Why dangerous:</i> a clerk in Ibadan could modify stock in Port Harcourt. <i>Fix:</i> look up the user's membership record and verify they hold <code>manager</code> or <code>clerk</code> permissions for <code>args.warehouseId</code>.</li><li><b>Untyped <code>v.string()</code> instead of <code>v.id()</code> (Validation):</b> <code>productId</code> and <code>warehouseId</code> use <code>v.string()</code>. <i>Why dangerous:</i> loses Convex table validation and allows arbitrary strings or swapped IDs. <i>Fix:</i> declare <code>productId: v.id("products")</code> and <code>warehouseId: v.id("warehouses")</code>.</li><li><b>No Existence Validation on Product and Warehouse (Validation):</b> the code never checks <code>await ctx.db.get(args.productId)</code> or <code>await ctx.db.get(args.warehouseId)</code>. <i>Why dangerous:</i> ghost inventory rows can be inserted for non-existent or deactivated warehouses/products. <i>Fix:</i> fetch both documents and throw <code>ConvexError("Product or warehouse not found")</code> if either is null.</li><li><b>Negative Inventory Allowed on Patch and Insert (Business Rules):</b> when <code>args.quantityChange</code> is negative (dispatching goods), <code>inventory.quantity + args.quantityChange</code> can drop below 0; worse, in the <code>else</code> branch, dispatching from an unstocked warehouse inserts a new row with a <b>negative</b> quantity! <i>Why dangerous:</i> allows dispatching stock that does not physically exist. <i>Fix:</i> check <code>const newQty = (inventory?.quantity ?? 0) + args.quantityChange; if (newQty &lt; 0) throw new ConvexError("Insufficient stock");</code> before writing.</li><li><b>No Integer / Non-Zero Check on <code>quantityChange</code> (Validation):</b> <code>v.number()</code> accepts <code>0</code>, <code>0.0004</code> or <code>-Infinity</code>. <i>Why dangerous:</i> corrupts discrete carton counts and wastes database writes on zero-change calls. <i>Fix:</i> validate <code>if (!Number.isInteger(args.quantityChange) || args.quantityChange === 0) throw new ConvexError("quantityChange must be a non-zero integer");</code>.</li><li><b>Full Table Scan with <code>.filter()</code> instead of <code>.withIndex()</code> (Performance):</b> <code>.query("inventory").filter(...)</code> scans every row across all 15 warehouses on every barcode scan. <i>Why inefficient:</i> as SKUs grow into tens of thousands, latency and read bandwidth degrade linearly and cause transaction contention. <i>Fix:</i> define <code>.index("by_warehouse_product", ["warehouseId", "productId"])</code> in <code>schema.ts</code> and query with <code>.withIndex("by_warehouse_product", q =&gt; q.eq("warehouseId", args.warehouseId).eq("productId", args.productId))</code>.</li><li><b>Using <code>.first()</code> instead of <code>.unique()</code> and No Stock Movement Audit Trail (Performance &amp; Business Integrity):</b> <code>.first()</code> silently ignores duplicate rows for the same product+warehouse, and overwriting <code>quantity</code> in place leaves zero audit trail of <i>who</i> dispatched stock and <i>when</i>. <i>Fix:</i> use <code>.unique()</code> on the compound index and insert an immutable ledger row into a <code>stockMovements</code> table (<code>userId, warehouseId, productId, quantityChange, reason, timestamp</code>) inside the same atomic mutation.</li></ol><p><b>Scenario:</b> when a Kano warehouse clerk scans 50 outbound cartons, the refactored mutation verifies their Kano role, looks up the exact row via <code>by_warehouse_product</code> with <code>.unique()</code>, confirms at least 50 cartons exist, patches the balance, and logs the movement atomically.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> <code>getUserIdentity()</code> and role checks secure access, <code>v.id()</code>, existence checks and non-negative integer rules protect business integrity, and <code>.withIndex(...).unique()</code> delivers O(1) indexed performance.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> directly applying the Backend Practical's Do/Don't table transforms a vulnerable prototype into an auditable, high-throughput multi-warehouse mutation.</p>`},{h:'Q2b · SmartInventory Testing Strategy',marks:5,q:'Explain your testing strategy for the inventory update functionality, including unit tests, integration tests, and edge cases you would test.',pts:['Unit tests: test validation & business-rule helpers in isolation (integer check, negative-stock calculation, role authorization check)','Integration tests: end-to-end Convex mutation against an in-memory/test database (receive new stock -> upsert insert; receive more -> patch increment; dispatch -> patch decrement + stockMovements audit row + reactive query update)','Edge cases: dispatching more than available balance (must throw ConvexError), dispatching a product with no existing inventory row, quantityChange = 0 or float/negative on insert, concurrent dispatches from two scanners in the same warehouse (atomic transaction isolation), unauthorized user or wrong warehouse role, non-existent productId/warehouseId'],model:`<p><b>Background:</b> as taught in the Backend Development lecture, testing verifies that backend code enforces rules and handles failures gracefully before deployment across three levels: unit tests, integration tests and edge cases.</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b></p><ul><li><b>1. Unit tests (functions and rules in isolation):</b> test pure validation and calculation helpers independently—for example, verifying that <code>calculateNewQuantity(currentQty, change)</code> rejects negative resulting balances, rejects fractional numbers like <code>2.5</code> or <code>0</code>, and that the permission helper correctly checks whether a user's warehouse assignment includes the target <code>warehouseId</code>.</li><li><b>2. Integration tests (components working together):</b> using a Convex test harness, execute full multi-step workflows across tables: seed a valid user, warehouse and product; call <code>updateInventory</code> with <code>+100</code> and assert a new row is inserted; call it again with <code>-30</code> and assert the row is patched to <code>70</code>, an audit record appears in <code>stockMovements</code>, and the warehouse inventory query reflects <code>70</code> immediately.</li><li><b>3. Edge cases (boundary and unusual conditions):</b><ul><li><b>Over-dispatch / zero boundary:</b> current stock is <code>10</code>, dispatch <code>-10</code> (succeeds, balance <code>0</code>) vs dispatch <code>-11</code> (throws <code>ConvexError("Insufficient stock")</code>).</li><li><b>Dispatching unstocked item:</b> calling <code>quantityChange: -5</code> when no inventory document exists yet for that product/warehouse.</li><li><b>Concurrent updates:</b> two barcode scanners in the Lagos warehouse simultaneously dispatching <code>-8</code> from a balance of <code>10</code>—verifying Convex's transactional mutation serializes them so the second fails cleanly instead of producing <code>-6</code>.</li><li><b>Invalid references &amp; auth boundaries:</b> deleted <code>productId</code>, unauthenticated caller, or an Abuja clerk attempting to update a Lagos <code>warehouseId</code>.</li></ul></li></ul><p><b>Scenario:</b> in CI/CD before deploying to the 15 Nigerian warehouses, the automated test suite runs all unit, integration and concurrency edge-case tests on every pull request and blocks merge if any negative-stock test passes erroneously.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> unit tests catch logic bugs in milliseconds, integration tests confirm schema indexes and database transactions work together, and edge-case tests harden the system against real-world warehouse race conditions.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> combining all three testing tiers guarantees that no stock update can bypass authentication, corrupt balances or fail silently under concurrent load.</p>`},{h:'Q3a · EduLearn RAG Architecture Deep Dive',marks:15,q:'Case Study: EduLearn is a personalised learning platform for 50,000 active students and 2,000 instructors with video lectures, assignments, quizzes, an AI study assistant, automated essay grading, and offline mode for poor internet.\n\nDesign a comprehensive RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) system for the AI study assistant that allows students to ask questions about course materials (PDFs, videos, lecture notes), provides accurate course-specific answers, and cites sources. Include:\n• The complete RAG pipeline (document processing, embedding, retrieval, generation)\n• Choice of vector database and embedding model with justification\n• Chunking strategy for different content types (PDFs vs video transcripts)\n• How you would prevent hallucinations and ensure answer quality\n• How you would handle the AI assistant saying "I don\'t know" when information isn\'t in the course materials\n• Specific tools/frameworks\n• Cost optimisation strategies',pts:['1. Complete RAG pipeline: ingestion/parsing (PDFs + Whisper video transcripts) -> structure-aware chunking with metadata (courseId, week, page/timestamp) -> embedding -> vector DB index -> query rewriting + metadata-filtered hybrid retrieval (vector + BM25) + reranking -> grounded LLM generation with inline citations','2. Vector DB & embedding model: Convex native vector search (or pgvector / Pinecone / Qdrant) filtered by courseId + text-embedding-3-small (or BGE-M3) for high accuracy per naira/dollar','3. Content-specific chunking: PDFs/notes chunked by semantic headings/slides (400-600 tokens, 10-15% overlap, keeping tables/code blocks intact + page metadata); Videos transcribed with Whisper and chunked by topic/time windows (60-120s with start/end timestamp metadata for deep-linking)','4. Preventing hallucinations: strict system prompt ("answer ONLY from provided context"), low temperature (0.0-0.2), mandatory inline citations [Slide 12, p.4] or [Video 04:20], cross-encoder reranking, and post-generation faithfulness/citation verification','5. Handling "I don\'t know": cosine similarity threshold cutoff before calling the LLM + abstention prompt rule ("If the answer is not in the retrieved course excerpts, state clearly that it is not covered in the uploaded course materials and suggest posting on the course discussion forum")','6. Specific tools/frameworks: Convex (actions + vectorIndex) or LangChain/LlamaIndex, PyMuPDF/Unstructured for PDFs, OpenAI Whisper for video lectures, Cohere Rerank','7. Cost optimisation for 50,000 students: semantic/exact query caching for repeated exam questions, tiered models (small fast model like GPT-4o-mini / Gemini Flash for Q&A), pre-filtering/cutoff to skip LLM calls on low similarity, batch async embedding on upload, token limits & rate limiting per student'],model:`<div class="trap"><b>Additional context:</b> RAG architecture is not in the 7 slide decks in the Blockchain folder, but it is a full 15-mark question on the Feb 2026 past exam paper.</div><p><b>Background:</b> a generic LLM lacks access to a university's private PDFs and video lectures and can hallucinate plausible-sounding wrong answers. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) grounds the assistant strictly in the student's enrolled course materials with verifiable page and timestamp citations.</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b></p><ol><li><b>Complete RAG Pipeline:</b><ul><li><b>Ingestion &amp; Document Processing:</b> when an instructor uploads a PDF, slide deck or lecture video, an asynchronous backend worker extracts clean text—using <b>PyMuPDF / Unstructured</b> for PDFs and <b>OpenAI Whisper</b> for video audio—attaching rich metadata (<code>courseId, moduleId, docTitle, pageNumber</code> or <code>startTimestamp/endTimestamp</code>).</li><li><b>Chunking &amp; Embedding:</b> text is split into structure-aware chunks, converted into dense vectors by an embedding model, and stored in the vector index alongside the raw text and metadata.</li><li><b>Retrieval:</b> when a student asks a question, a Convex <code>action</code> embeds the query, filters strictly by the student's <code>courseId</code> (so CSC 419 never retrieves COS 409 notes), runs <b>hybrid search</b> (vector similarity + keyword BM25 for exact code/acronyms), and passes the top 15 hits through a <b>Reranker</b> to keep the top 4–5 chunks.</li><li><b>Generation:</b> the top chunks and their citation tags are injected into a grounded prompt; the LLM generates the answer with clickable citations.</li></ul></li><li><b>Vector Database &amp; Embedding Model:</b> use <b>Convex Vector Search</b> (or PostgreSQL <b>pgvector</b> / <b>Qdrant</b>) because it lives directly alongside EduLearn's course enrollment and authorization tables and supports native metadata filtering by <code>courseId</code>. Use <b>OpenAI <code>text-embedding-3-small</code></b> (1536 dims, or open-source <b>BGE-M3</b>): it offers strong retrieval accuracy at roughly 5x lower cost than large embedding models—crucial at 50,000-student scale.</li><li><b>Chunking Strategy (PDFs vs Video Transcripts):</b><ul><li><b>PDFs &amp; Slide Decks:</b> use <b>layout/markdown-aware recursive chunking</b> (400–600 tokens with 50-token overlap), splitting on slide boundaries or section headings while keeping code blocks and tables unbroken. Metadata stores <code>{ sourceType: "pdf", fileName, pageNumber, heading }</code>.</li><li><b>Video Lectures:</b> transcribe with word-level timestamps and chunk by <b>speaker pauses / topic shifts over 60–120 second windows</b> (~300–500 tokens with 15-second overlap). Metadata stores <code>{ sourceType: "video", videoId, startSec, endSec }</code> so clicking a citation seeks the video player directly to that timestamp.</li></ul></li><li><b>Preventing Hallucinations &amp; Ensuring Quality:</b> set temperature to <b>0.0–0.1</b>; use a strict system prompt requiring every factual claim to cite a retrieved <code>[Chunk ID]</code>; use hybrid search + <b>Cohere Rerank</b> so noisy chunks are dropped; and validate in code that every cited <code>[Chunk ID]</code> actually exists in the retrieved set.</li><li><b>Handling "I Don't Know" (Abstention):</b> enforce a <b>two-stage abstention gate</b>: (a) <i>Pre-LLM threshold:</i> if the top retrieved chunk's similarity/rerank score is below a calibrated threshold (e.g. <code>0.68</code>), return a canned response immediately without calling the LLM; (b) <i>Prompt rule:</i> instruct the LLM: <i>"If the provided excerpts do not contain the answer, respond: 'I couldn't find this in your course materials for [Course]. Would you like to post this question to the course discussion forum?'"</i></li><li><b>Specific Tools &amp; Frameworks:</b> <b>LlamaIndex</b> or <b>LangChain</b> for ingestion orchestration, <b>PyMuPDF / Unstructured.io</b> (PDFs), <b>Whisper</b> (video transcription), <b>Convex Actions + Vector Index</b> (or Qdrant/pgvector), <b>Cohere Rerank</b>, and <b>GPT-4o-mini / Gemini 1.5 Flash</b> for fast generation.</li><li><b>Cost Optimisation for 50,000 Students:</b> (a) <b>Semantic &amp; exact caching</b> (Redis / Convex cache table): during exam week thousands of students ask identical questions about the same slide—serve cached answers instantly at zero token cost; (b) <b>Pre-LLM similarity cutoff</b> so off-topic queries don't trigger LLM tokens; (c) <b>Small frontier models</b> (GPT-4o-mini / Flash) for standard Q&amp;A; (d) <b>Embed once on instructor upload</b> (never re-embed per student); and (e) <b>Per-student rate limiting and usage quotas</b> (e.g. 50 queries/day) as taught in the Backend Scalability slides.</li></ol><p><b>Scenario:</b> a student on a slow connection asks EduLearn <i>"Why shouldn't we use .filter() in Convex?"</i>; the retriever filters by <code>courseId="CSC419"</code>, pulls page 11 of <code>Backend Practical Part 1.pdf</code>, checks the semantic cache, and returns the exact answer citing <i>[Backend Practical Part 1, p. 11]</i>.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> modality-specific chunking preserves page and video-time metadata, hybrid retrieval + reranking supplies high-precision context, similarity cutoffs enforce honest "I don't know" replies, and semantic caching keeps costs sustainable for 50,000 students.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> a production educational RAG system wins on retrieval precision, strict citation grounding and aggressive caching rather than raw LLM size.</p>`},{h:'Q4a · VoiceBank Backend API & Security Design',marks:5,q:'Case Study: VoiceBank is a mobile-first banking platform targeting Nigeria\'s 60 million unbanked adults with voice-based navigation in Pidgin, Yoruba, Igbo and Hausa, face recognition auth (no PINs), AI micro-loans based on phone usage patterns, rural cash agent network, mobile money/crypto integration, and automated savings.\n\nDesign the backend system addressing API Design: list 8 critical API endpoints with authentication/authorisation requirements.',pts:['1. POST /api/auth/biometric-enroll & verify (Public/Device-attested -> issues short-lived JWT + refresh token; liveness check + rate limit)','2. GET /api/accounts/me/balance (Authenticated Customer or assigned Agent with customer token; Owner/Customer role only)','3. POST /api/voice/intent (Authenticated Customer; accepts audio/transcript in Pidgin/Yoruba/Igbo/Hausa, returns parsed banking intent; read-only until confirmed)','4. POST /api/transactions/transfer (Authenticated Customer + step-up biometric/voice confirmation token + idempotency key; Owner role)','5. POST /api/agents/cash-in-out (Authenticated & KYC-verified Rural Agent role + Customer biometric/OTP authorization; territory & daily float limits)','6. POST /api/loans/apply & GET /api/loans/:id/decision (Authenticated Customer; triggers async credit-scoring action; Compliance/Underwriter role for manual overrides)','7. POST /api/savings/rules (Authenticated Customer Owner role; configures automated savings caps and pause controls)','8. GET /api/admin/audit-logs (Internal Compliance / CBN Auditor role only; read-only immutable transaction ledger)'],model:`<p><b>Background:</b> as taught in the Backend Development slides, APIs are the contract between frontend and backend, and every endpoint handling sensitive data must enforce authentication (verifying identity) and role-based authorization (verifying permissions) alongside rate limiting and input validation.</p><p><b>Answer and solution (8 critical VoiceBank endpoints and their Auth/Authz rules):</b></p><ol><li><b><code>POST /api/auth/face-verify</code> (Authentication &amp; Liveness):</b> accepts an encrypted facial embedding + hardware liveness attestation from the mobile app. <i>Auth/Authz:</i> device-signed request with strict IP/device rate limiting (max 5 attempts/15 min); validates liveness and issues a short-lived signed JWT session token.</li><li><b><code>POST /api/voice/interpret</code> (Multilingual Voice Command Parser):</b> accepts voice audio in Pidgin, Yoruba, Igbo or Hausa and returns a structured intent preview (e.g. <code>{ action: "TRANSFER", amount: 5000, recipient: "..." }</code>). <i>Auth/Authz:</i> requires valid Customer JWT (<b>Customer Owner</b> role); strictly <b>read-only preview</b>—never moves money without the confirmation endpoint.</li><li><b><code>GET /api/accounts/:accountId/summary</code> (Balance &amp; History):</b> returns balance and paginated ledger entries. <i>Auth/Authz:</i> requires Customer JWT; authorization checks <code>account.ownerId === identity.userId</code> (or <b>Auditor/Support</b> role with masked sensitive fields).</li><li><b><code>POST /api/transactions/transfer</code> (Funds Transfer):</b> executes an atomic debit/credit transfer. <i>Auth/Authz:</i> requires Customer JWT (<b>Owner</b> role) + a single-use <b>step-up biometric confirmation token</b> + an <code>Idempotency-Key</code> header to prevent double-debits on poor rural 2G/3G networks.</li><li><b><code>POST /api/agents/cash-deposit-withdrawal</code> (Rural Agent Cash-In / Cash-Out):</b> logs physical cash deposits or withdrawals at a village agent kiosk. <i>Auth/Authz:</i> dual authorization—requires a verified <b>Agent</b> role JWT (checked against agent float limit and geofence) <b>plus</b> the customer's live face-verification signature.</li><li><b><code>POST /api/loans/micro-apply</code> (AI Micro-Loan Application):</b> submits a loan request and consented telemetry for scoring. <i>Auth/Authz:</i> requires Customer JWT (<b>Owner</b> role) with explicit data-consent flag; enqueues an async scoring job and enforces cooling-off and max-debt caps.</li><li><b><code>PUT /api/savings/auto-rules</code> (Automated Savings Plan):</b> configures or pauses AI-triggered savings deductions. <i>Auth/Authz:</i> requires Customer JWT (<b>Owner</b> role); validates maximum deduction percentage so essential living funds are never swept without bounds.</li><li><b><code>GET /api/compliance/audit-trail</code> (Regulatory &amp; Fraud Audit):</b> exports immutable transaction and AI-decision logs. <i>Auth/Authz:</i> restricted strictly to internal <b>Compliance Officer / CBN Auditor</b> roles with MFA (returns <code>403 Forbidden</code> to all customer and agent tokens).</li></ol><p><b>Scenario:</b> when a market trader in Aba says in Igbo <i>"Send 3,000 naira to Chinedu"</i>, <code>/api/voice/interpret</code> parses the intent, reads it back in Igbo for confirmation, and only calls <code>/api/transactions/transfer</code> after a fresh face-liveness token and idempotency key are attached.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> separating read-only voice parsing from step-up authenticated money movement prevents voice-spoofing theft, while dual agent-customer authorization prevents rural agent fraud.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> every financial endpoint must pair identity authentication with strict resource-ownership authorization, idempotency and rate limits.</p>`},{h:'Q4b · AI Credit Scoring Ethical & Bias Analysis',marks:5,q:'AI Implementation & Risk — Feature 2 (AI Credit Scoring): The system analyses phone usage (calls, data, app usage, location) to determine creditworthiness. Provide a critical analysis addressing:\n• What are the ethical implications of this approach?\n• What biases might the AI introduce?\n• Could this perpetuate existing inequalities?\n• How would you ensure fairness and transparency?\n• Should this feature be built at all? Defend your position.',pts:['Ethical implications: invasive surveillance (location, call logs, apps) of vulnerable low-literacy users who cannot meaningfully read dense privacy policies; function creep','Biases introduced: proxy discrimination against rural residents, women, elderly or low-income workers (shared family phones, intermittent electricity/charging, 2G coverage gaps, less mobile data spend)','Perpetuating inequalities: charges higher interest or denies credit to the poorest unbanked Nigerians solely because their phone telemetry looks "sparse", entrenching the poverty trap','Fairness & transparency: drop invasive location/call-spy telemetry in favour of explainable cash-flow/agent-savings history, translate plain-language explanations into Pidgin/Yoruba/Igbo/Hausa voice, audit disparate impact across gender/region, provide human appeal path','Defended position: do NOT build raw phone-surveillance scoring as proposed; replace it with graduated, opt-in relationship lending based on verified agent cash-in/savings behaviour and community/cooperative history'],model:`<div class="trap"><b>Additional context:</b> AI fairness and credit-scoring ethics connect to computing ethics principles beyond the 7 technical slide decks.</div><p><b>Background:</b> alternative credit scoring aims to lend to people without formal payslips or bank statements, but scraping intimate smartphone telemetry (calls, app usage, GPS location) from 60 million unbanked Nigerians raises severe privacy, bias and justice concerns.</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b></p><ul><li><b>1. Ethical implications:</b> pervasive surveillance and <b>coerced consent</b>—forcing low-income citizens to surrender their call logs and real-time location just to access basic micro-credit violates privacy and autonomy, especially when low-literacy users cannot audit how that data is shared or monetized.</li><li><b>2. Biases the AI introduces:</b> smartphone metadata is a noisy, biased proxy for character or repayment capacity. Rural farmers with weak network coverage, irregular power for charging, or basic feature phones—and women who share a household handset—generate fewer data sessions and "irregular" location patterns, causing the model to misclassify them as high-risk.</li><li><b>3. Perpetuating existing inequalities:</b> urban, wealthier users with steady 4G data and expensive smartphones receive cheap credit, while the poorest rural citizens are either denied loans or charged punitive "high-risk" interest rates, widening Nigeria's urban–rural and gender wealth divide.</li><li><b>4. Ensuring fairness and transparency:</b> strip out invasive GPS/call-log spying; use explainable models (e.g. monotonic gradient boosting or scorecards) trained on transparent financial behaviour (agent deposits, utility top-ups, cooperative savings); run regular demographic parity audits across regions and languages; speak the exact reason for any denial aloud in the user's language (Pidgin/Yoruba/Igbo/Hausa); and guarantee a human agent review option.</li><li><b>5. Should this feature be built at all? (Position):</b> <b>No—not in the form of phone-surveillance scoring.</b> It should be redesigned into a <b>graduated savings-and-repayment ladder</b> (starting with tiny starter limits backed by the user's VoiceBank agent cash-in history and growing as they repay on time), which builds creditworthiness transparently without spying on private phone life.</li></ul><p><b>Scenario:</b> a rural cassava trader in Benue turns her phone off all day to save battery where there is no grid power; a naive phone-usage AI rejects her loan for "low digital activity", whereas a graduated agent-deposit scorecard sees her steady weekly cash deposits and approves her fairly.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> data minimisation protects privacy, explainable cash-flow features eliminate proxy bias against rural hardware constraints, and multilingual voice explanations uphold transparency.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> financial inclusion must not become digital surveillance; replace invasive phone telemetry with transparent, graduated cash-flow underwriting.</p>`},{h:'Q4c · CBN Regulator Reality Check (Viability & Ethics)',marks:5,q:'Address these concerns from a sceptical CBN (Central Bank of Nigeria) regulator:\n• Trust & Adoption: Why would people who don\'t trust traditional banks trust an AI? Many unbanked Nigerians actively choose cash. How do you address this?\n• Infrastructure Dependencies: Face recognition requires good lighting and cameras. Voice requires quiet environments and data. Aren\'t you just excluding people differently?\n• Predatory Lending: Your AI micro-loans could trap vulnerable people in debt cycles. What safeguards prevent this?\n• Data Privacy: You\'re collecting incredibly sensitive data (financial + biometric + location + behaviour). In Nigeria\'s current regulatory environment, how do you protect users?\n• Sustainability: How does VoiceBank make money without exploiting the poor? If the startup fails, what happens to people\'s savings?\nProvide a balanced assessment concluding whether VoiceBank should proceed as planned, be significantly modified, or abandoned.',pts:['1. Trust & Adoption: human-in-the-loop rural agent network (trusted local shopkeepers), instant paper/SMS receipts, AI as a voice helper rather than an opaque decision-maker, zero hidden fees','2. Infrastructure Dependencies: offline-first / USSD / IVR toll-free voice call fallback for feature phones and 2G areas, noise-robust voice + mandatory PIN/agent-assisted fallback when cameras or lighting fail','3. Predatory Lending safeguards: hard interest-rate caps, no compounding rollover traps, cooling-off periods, debt-to-income limits, no contact-list shaming, financial literacy prompts before borrowing','4. Data Privacy (NDPA 2023 & CBN compliance): on-device biometric template hashing, strict data minimisation (drop location/call scraping), AES-256/TLS encryption, local data residency, voice-based consent revocability','5. Sustainability & Depositor Protection: revenue from B2B merchant/agent float fees and low-fee micro-insurance/remittances (not loan-shark interest); partner with a CBN-licensed Microfinance/Commercial Bank so all savings sit in NDIC-insured custodial accounts ring-fenced from startup bankruptcy + verdict: significantly modified'],model:`<p><b>Background:</b> deploying fintech to 60 million unbanked Nigerians is as much a regulatory, infrastructural and ethical challenge as a software challenge. A credible architecture must satisfy Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN) consumer-protection rules, the Nigeria Data Protection Act (NDPA 2023) and the harsh realities of rural power and connectivity.</p><p><b>Answer and solution (Addressing the 5 CBN regulator concerns):</b></p><ol><li><b>Trust &amp; Adoption:</b> unbanked citizens trust people, not black-box AI. Anchor adoption in the <b>local rural agent network</b> (trusted community shopkeepers), provide physical/SMS transaction receipts alongside voice readbacks in Pidgin/Yoruba/Igbo/Hausa, keep the AI strictly as a conversational interface (never moving money without explicit user confirmation), and display transparent zero-hidden-fee pricing.</li><li><b>Infrastructure Dependencies:</b> a smartphone-only app with high-res face recognition and streaming voice AI will exclude the very people who only own N10,000 feature phones in noisy markets with 2G networks. Provide a <b>multi-channel fallback</b>: toll-free <b>IVR voice calls and USSD</b> for feature phones, edge-compressed audio for noisy markets, and a <b>4–6 digit spoken/keypad PIN + agent-assisted verification fallback</b> whenever camera lighting or face recognition fails.</li><li><b>Predatory Lending Safeguards:</b> enforce CBN-aligned <b>interest-rate and fee caps</b> (total cost of credit never exceeding principal rules), ban automatic loan rollovers and contact-list defamation/harassment, cap automated savings sweeps and loan repayments at a safe fraction of observed cash inflows, and require a short vernacular audio explanation of total repayment before disbursement.</li><li><b>Data Privacy (NDPA 2023 Compliance):</b> drop continuous GPS and call-log harvesting (data minimisation); store facial biometrics as non-reversible mathematical templates encrypted at rest (AES-256) and in transit (TLS 1.3); host regulated financial records within CBN-approved jurisdictions; drop speculative cryptocurrency integrations that violate CBN banking restrictions; and let users revoke consent by voice command.</li><li><b>Sustainability &amp; Ring-Fenced Savings Protection:</b> earn revenue from transparent agent/merchant transaction commissions, payroll/cooperative float and B2B remittance rails rather than predatory micro-loan interest. Crucially, hold all customer deposits in <b>NDIC-insured custodial accounts at a partnered CBN-licensed commercial or microfinance bank</b>—so if the VoiceBank startup fails tomorrow, every customer's savings remain 100% intact and withdrawable at the partner bank.</li></ol><p><b>Scenario:</b> when a farmer in Sokoto without a smartphone dials VoiceBank's toll-free IVR line on a basic Nokia phone, he checks his NDIC-insured savings balance in Hausa and withdraws cash at his village agent using a PIN fallback without needing 4G data or a selfie camera.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> IVR/USSD + PIN fallbacks solve hardware exclusion, NDPA data minimisation and rate caps prevent exploitation, and NDIC-insured custodial banking protects deposits from startup failure.</p><p><b>Conclusion (Final Verdict):</b> VoiceBank should <b>NOT proceed as originally planned, nor be abandoned—it must be significantly modified</b>: drop crypto and invasive phone-spy credit scoring, add USSD/IVR and PIN fallbacks alongside voice/face biometrics, and ring-fence all deposits inside an NDIC-insured, CBN-licensed partner bank.</p>`}]}
};
